  ],
  "activity_sections": [
    {
      "key": "2e5cd3eb-78fd-4b43-8960-af3457fa5d14",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students share something they know a lot about with a small group, building early comfort speaking up and previewing the idea that everyone brings valuable knowledge to this course."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2e5cd3eb-78fd-4b43-8960-af3457fa5d14",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20a06be8-12f9-4127-8d31-496852b5ae31"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3174bad8-32f6-45b2-ae68-fca399d2761e",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Welcome to Computer Science Principles! This year we’re going to learn a lot about computer science, but before we begin, we want to learn a little bit about you and your thoughts on computer science in general. Let’s start by seeing how creative we can be right now!",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3174bad8-32f6-45b2-ae68-fca399d2761e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20a06be8-12f9-4127-8d31-496852b5ae31"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "02cf62da-a911-44f7-bdbe-73ba48b4a36c",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into groups of 2-4. Direct students to introduce themselves while you distribute sticky notes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a sticky note or scratch paper."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "02cf62da-a911-44f7-bdbe-73ba48b4a36c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20a06be8-12f9-4127-8d31-496852b5ae31"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ed04ae7a-47f5-40af-b650-ec16a0f06f98",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students a minute to write down something that they know a lot about or could teach someone. Once students are ready, click the animated slide to reveal the next steps: each person in the group should share what they wrote down on their sticky and then tell the group something interesting about that topic.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for topics that might resurface later in the course, and prompt any hesitant students with a follow-up question about what they shared.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The goal is for students to name something they're interested in and know enough about to share real insight — it doesn't have to be a school subject. Much of their willingness to open up will come from your own genuine curiosity about what they share. If students get stuck, remind them everyone has some hobby, interest, or hidden talent worth talking about."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ed04ae7a-47f5-40af-b650-ec16a0f06f98",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20a06be8-12f9-4127-8d31-496852b5ae31"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b47b57a8-6906-40e9-9d1e-cd15a8199909",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b47b57a8-6906-40e9-9d1e-cd15a8199909",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20a06be8-12f9-4127-8d31-496852b5ae31"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e6bbb848-e947-42aa-9877-42209fe66ab4",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e6bbb848-e947-42aa-9877-42209fe66ab4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20a06be8-12f9-4127-8d31-496852b5ae31"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7a10da51-b5d2-4e01-baca-0ca7cd542e04",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students identify how technology touches an area they're interested in and sketch out a creative way it could be improved or a new problem it could solve, practicing the mindset of noticing problems and imagining solutions.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Rapid Prototype an Innovation"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7a10da51-b5d2-4e01-baca-0ca7cd542e04",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3c27a5fe-de5f-4592-b768-24e395c986a2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1da8d283-3f4f-478b-bcce-de293f15aeb5",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Technology seems to be everywhere, affecting everything we do. But it starts with someone recognizing a problem and building something to solve it. As a class, we're going to try \"rapid prototyping\" — a prototype is just a quick sketch of an idea, the first draft something real might be built from.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1da8d283-3f4f-478b-bcce-de293f15aeb5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3c27a5fe-de5f-4592-b768-24e395c986a2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "97f82043-efe1-41ba-b9af-f7eb61e12182",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each group a poster paper."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "97f82043-efe1-41ba-b9af-f7eb61e12182",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3c27a5fe-de5f-4592-b768-24e395c986a2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "37adaab8-69ee-47a4-a0fe-cae2fcf22738",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have each group work through their members' areas of interest, brainstorming how technology touches each one and one way it could be improved.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that every group member is contributing ideas across all the interests in their group, not just their own.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Keep things quick. If a group is worried about not being innovative enough, remind them that very small ideas can have big consequences. People once thought it was ridiculous that you would want to send a short text message to another person over a phone.\n\nAlternatively, a group may have a great idea that they want to spend more time on. They can do that later. For now, just remind them it's a rapid prototype."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "37adaab8-69ee-47a4-a0fe-cae2fcf22738",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3c27a5fe-de5f-4592-b768-24e395c986a2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6192f2e3-e9af-4bc8-8dd8-6e57f47f38dd",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct groups to agree on the idea from their brainstorm that would be most interesting to the rest of the class, sketch it on their poster, and jot down one possible downside or new problem their idea could create.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As groups work, prompt them to think beyond the benefit — ask \"what's one way this could go wrong, or create a new problem?\" if they're stuck.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If a group starts perfecting their drawing, remind them this is still a rapid prototype — a rough sketch is enough. Polish isn't the goal; conveying the idea clearly is."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use the completed posters as a quick formative check — do students' sketches show a technology idea connected to a real interest, and does their noted downside or new problem show genuine thinking about effects, not just a token answer? This can help you gauge how well the class is grasping both lesson objectives before moving forward."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6192f2e3-e9af-4bc8-8dd8-6e57f47f38dd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3c27a5fe-de5f-4592-b768-24e395c986a2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bbd9bd61-78ac-4883-b24e-9e2179173fe5",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students put their posters on the wall. Do a \"Gallery Walk\" or a whip around so that each student can see all of the other groups' work.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Take this opportunity to explain the importance of bringing individual interests and perspectives to this course. From day one, students should be thinking about how to apply the principles they learn to their own lives — hopefully with excitement."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bbd9bd61-78ac-4883-b24e-9e2179173fe5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3c27a5fe-de5f-4592-b768-24e395c986a2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "983ab559-b8b8-4432-bc6c-1e8c59fc3917",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students see how computer science touches a wide range of fields beyond what they might expect, connecting back to the prototyping they just did.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Making Connections"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "983ab559-b8b8-4432-bc6c-1e8c59fc3917",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3c27a5fe-de5f-4592-b768-24e395c986a2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "75bf5f2c-53f8-4be8-829d-8561c50d5ad1",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We're just starting this class, but you all bring passions and knowledge about things you care about — and it's likely that those things involve technology in some way. Everyone's unique, but we're all in this together.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "75bf5f2c-53f8-4be8-829d-8561c50d5ad1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3c27a5fe-de5f-4592-b768-24e395c986a2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a866421d-5e8e-42d1-81d6-4dac4afe2e59",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-video\"></i> **Video:** Show the \"Computer Science is Changing Everything\" video.\n\n**Questions to consider with the video:**\n- What are you excited to learn about in this course?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students can discuss in small groups and then share out with the whole class. It's ok if at this point students don't have an answer to this prompt."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a866421d-5e8e-42d1-81d6-4dac4afe2e59",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3c27a5fe-de5f-4592-b768-24e395c986a2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "77541068-2cf6-42a6-982e-ad772184a25d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "77541068-2cf6-42a6-982e-ad772184a25d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c3bc5127-6d31-4ab6-aa4b-88120df0041b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "85a57e33-af19-46b2-9c3d-1670be0e089a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Read the Question of the Day, then click the animated slide to display the two examples on the slide, asking students to raise their hand if they've felt that problem themselves. Invite one or two volunteers to share an app idea of their own. Keep this quick and low-pressure — the goal is a fun, casual close to the period, not a deep discussion."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "85a57e33-af19-46b2-9c3d-1670be0e089a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c3bc5127-6d31-4ab6-aa4b-88120df0041b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "411a7d56-b2c3-4b4f-8e87-f1d731ebfa77",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Teachers and students should complete the end of lesson survey.",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "411a7d56-b2c3-4b4f-8e87-f1d731ebfa77",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c3bc5127-6d31-4ab6-aa4b-88120df0041b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "91534293-33fa-4d62-a893-e9f30095e700",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Imagine you have to teach a computer to spot fish in the ocean, but the only way you can tell it anything is by writing rules. Make your rules specific enough that a computer could follow them with no guessing.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "91534293-33fa-4d62-a893-e9f30095e700",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2e1d6f8f-dbce-418c-9169-25dcac508911"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7fc00135-e63d-4f99-8f5e-73467ae2d0e0",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students write three rules on scratch paper. Give them 2 minutes to write silently, then 1 minute to compare with a neighbor."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7fc00135-e63d-4f99-8f5e-73467ae2d0e0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2e1d6f8f-dbce-418c-9169-25dcac508911"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9add3f9f-3c0c-4b69-bb44-07c1beb8c0c5",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reveal the pictures and give students 2 minutes to check each one against their three rules.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Which pictures broke your rules?*\n- *Did any rule accept something that is not a fish, or reject a real fish?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that rules written by people miss cases nobody planned for, and that fixing one rule can break another. Many students' rules will overlap (lives in water, has fins, swims), which shows how a few shared assumptions can all fail on the same unexpected picture.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students say the rules just need to be more detailed, ask, \"What rule would you add for the dolphin? Would it break for any real fish?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9add3f9f-3c0c-4b69-bb44-07c1beb8c0c5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2e1d6f8f-dbce-418c-9169-25dcac508911"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "26bade5a-0b97-43bb-9c74-05155fcb2fbd",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "26bade5a-0b97-43bb-9c74-05155fcb2fbd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2e1d6f8f-dbce-418c-9169-25dcac508911"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b5d0f409-199c-465c-bd42-efd51ba23a1e",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b5d0f409-199c-465c-bd42-efd51ba23a1e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2e1d6f8f-dbce-418c-9169-25dcac508911"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0c1d7896-8e10-4cb4-a1ed-45aec9361e71",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students train an A.I. with their own labels so they experience how a model learns from examples, then give them the vocabulary to describe it: AI system, model, and the difference between rules-based and learned behavior.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Instruct students to take out their copy of the CSP Unit Guide.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Train the AI"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0c1d7896-8e10-4cb4-a1ed-45aec9361e71",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fca6c6b7-d233-4989-aacc-f6796e6621c3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fc18b8c7-ad42-4036-b01c-d193b7ce0cbe",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-video\"></i> **Video:** Play the video \"What is Machine Learning\".",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "**Alternatives to AI For Oceans:** AI for Oceans was originally developed as an Hour of Code activity that can be completed by students with any device available. We have modified it for its usage here. Depending on your classroom situation, you might opt to replace the activity with:\n\n**Teachable Machine** - *Teachable Machine is a web-based tool that makes creating machine learning models fast, easy, and accessible to everyone. Teachable Machine is flexible – use files or capture examples live. It’s respectful of the way you work. You can even choose to use it entirely on-device, without any webcam or microphone data leaving your computer.*\n\nIf your classrooms devices have cameras, Teachable Machines offers an engaging way to create training sets. Encourage students to teach the machine to represent rock, paper, or scissors with hand gestures. What are some possible ways for bias to enter in?\n\n**Machine Learning for Kids** - *This free tool introduces machine learning by providing hands-on experiences for training machine learning systems and building things with them. It provides an easy-to-use guided environment for training machine learning models to recognise text, numbers, images, or sounds.*\n\nMachine Learning for Kids is a great option if your students want to work with text samples. Teach the machine to recognize words or passages that are happy or sad. Lots to play around with here!"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fc18b8c7-ad42-4036-b01c-d193b7ce0cbe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fca6c6b7-d233-4989-aacc-f6796e6621c3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9c6a78b8-b306-44ea-b74b-e77f9f0a4778",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Machine learning refers to a computer that can recognize patterns and make decisions. In this activity you’re going to supply the data to train your own machine learning model. Imagine an ocean that contains creatures like fish, but also contains trash dumped by humans. What if we could train a computer to tell the difference and then use that technology to help clean the ocean?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9c6a78b8-b306-44ea-b74b-e77f9f0a4778",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fca6c6b7-d233-4989-aacc-f6796e6621c3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ca45f2d1-ae4a-4bc6-b431-9565f3e71a2c",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 1-3. Students should spend around five minutes total on these levels.\n- **Level 1:** Students train the AI to recognize \"fish\" or \"not fish\"\n- **Level 2:** Students see what happens when their training from level 1 is used to determine if other sea creatures belong in the ocean.\n- **Level 3:** Students train the AI on the broader question of what belongs in the water.\n\n",
        "progression_name": "Train the AI",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Prompt their thinking with the question \"What potential problems do you think come up when creating training sets?\""
          },
          {
            "type": "contentCorner",
            "markdown": "Every image in this part of the tutorial is fed into a neural network that has been pre-trained on a huge set of data called ImageNet. The database contains over 14 million hand-annotated images. ImageNet contains more than 20,000 categories with a typical category, such as \"balloon\" or \"strawberry\", consisting of several hundred images. When A.I. is scanning new images and making its own predictions in the tutorial, it is actually comparing the possible categories for the new image with the patterns it found in the training dataset."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ca45f2d1-ae4a-4bc6-b431-9565f3e71a2c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fca6c6b7-d233-4989-aacc-f6796e6621c3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "916e57f9-6ceb-413f-adb7-ac4c3c9950b3",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *How well did A.I. do?*\n- *How do you think it decided what to include in the ocean?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that the A.I. only knew what their labels taught it. Answers about how well it did will vary, so the aim is to connect the results to the examples they supplied."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "916e57f9-6ceb-413f-adb7-ac4c3c9950b3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fca6c6b7-d233-4989-aacc-f6796e6621c3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "07ccdf94-da12-4169-9ecc-dca8f39143ba",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-video\"></i> **Video:** Play the video \"Training Data & Bias\"."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "07ccdf94-da12-4169-9ecc-dca8f39143ba",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fca6c6b7-d233-4989-aacc-f6796e6621c3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "203d3f56-463e-466c-bc90-f50e15455821",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** How do you think your training data influenced the results that A.I. produced?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that the A.I. could only learn from the examples they gave it. When the training data left out sea creatures, the A.I. called them \"not fish,\" so students' choices as trainers shaped what it got right and wrong."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "203d3f56-463e-466c-bc90-f50e15455821",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fca6c6b7-d233-4989-aacc-f6796e6621c3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "328bf16f-7446-4361-b15a-56532900e229",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The fish-finder you just trained is an AI system. It took in pictures and produced a decision: fish or not fish.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "328bf16f-7446-4361-b15a-56532900e229",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fca6c6b7-d233-4989-aacc-f6796e6621c3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8207d405-290c-455a-8d43-f8c0fb99319e",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *AI System*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8207d405-290c-455a-8d43-f8c0fb99319e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fca6c6b7-d233-4989-aacc-f6796e6621c3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3a544063-dd8e-4103-89b4-b75e29c97e6f",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Inside the fish-finder, the model is the part that looks at a picture and decides. Your warm-up rules were one kind of model. The one you trained with labels is another kind.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3a544063-dd8e-4103-89b4-b75e29c97e6f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fca6c6b7-d233-4989-aacc-f6796e6621c3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "227e45b5-f0ba-41bc-9430-0c1ba74f5a21",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *model*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "227e45b5-f0ba-41bc-9430-0c1ba74f5a21",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fca6c6b7-d233-4989-aacc-f6796e6621c3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "19808bd6-ea89-4030-a20d-76356a6f9aca",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Remember when the trained A.I. called sea creatures \"not fish\"? It had no examples of them. That is the learned version of a rule nobody wrote.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "19808bd6-ea89-4030-a20d-76356a6f9aca",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fca6c6b7-d233-4989-aacc-f6796e6621c3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e238a1b1-53c6-4db3-a880-adeae39fbfb3",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the table using students' own warm-up rules and the Levels 1-3 results.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Learned models still depend on human choices, because people pick the examples and the labels. That sets up the bias discussion that comes next."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e238a1b1-53c6-4db3-a880-adeae39fbfb3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fca6c6b7-d233-4989-aacc-f6796e6621c3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a2c55674-87df-4cce-bc4d-50d5503cd3c9",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students train an A.I. on a word that depends on opinion so they see their own judgments become the training data. Help them connect that experience to real computing innovations and to what designers and developers can do to reduce bias and consider the effects of their programs.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Bias in Training Data"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a2c55674-87df-4cce-bc4d-50d5503cd3c9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fca6c6b7-d233-4989-aacc-f6796e6621c3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3dc68241-89ca-40c8-8e41-bc78e59a4b6d",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now you will teach A.I. about a word of your choosing by showing it examples of that type of fish. As before, A.I. doesn't start with any training data about these labels. Even though the words in this level are fairly objective, it's possible that you will end up with different results based on their training data. You might even intentionally train A.I. incorrectly to see what happens!",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3dc68241-89ca-40c8-8e41-bc78e59a4b6d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fca6c6b7-d233-4989-aacc-f6796e6621c3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d17fb5df-07ad-45b3-8bd5-8fbcc0edd771",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 4-5. Students should spend around five minutes total on these levels.\n- **Level 4:** Students train the AI to recognize a new word of their choice.\n- **Level 5:** Students train the AI on a new, subjective word of their choice.\n\n",
        "progression_name": "Bias In Training Data",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Prompt their thinking with the question \"Is it fair to use AI to judge something by looks? What unintended bias could this cause?\" Here, as before, students will use training data to teach A.I. to recognize different types of fish. The words in this list are intentionally more subjective than what students will have seen so far. Encourage students to decide for themselves what makes a fish look \"angry\" or \"fun\". Two students may choose the same label and get a very different set of results based on which fish traits were their focus. Encourage students to discuss their findings with each other or go back and choose new words. Each student will rely on their own opinions to train A.I. which means that A.I. will learn with the same biases held by the students. As students begin to see the role their opinion is playing, ask them to reflect on whether this is good or bad, and how it might be addressed."
          },
          {
            "type": "contentCorner",
            "markdown": "The fish in this tutorial are randomly generated based on some pre-defined components, including mouths, tails, eyes, scales, and fins, with a randomly chosen body color, shape, and size. Rather than looking at the actual image data, A.I. is now looking for patterns in these components based on how the student classifies each fish. It will be more likely to label a fish the same way the student would have if it has matching traits."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d17fb5df-07ad-45b3-8bd5-8fbcc0edd771",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fca6c6b7-d233-4989-aacc-f6796e6621c3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b181ac69-488e-4238-bac5-80cbc6bcb121",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-video\"></i> **Video:** Play the video \"How I’m Fighting Bias in Algorithms\" with Joy Buolamwini.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This clip stops before Joy's recommendations. If students ask what can be done about algorithmic bias, share her three ideas: who codes matters (diverse teams who can check each other's blind spots), how we code matters (building fairness into the system), and why we code matters (making social change a priority)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b181ac69-488e-4238-bac5-80cbc6bcb121",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fca6c6b7-d233-4989-aacc-f6796e6621c3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "efd5752d-a344-46f2-b724-2a915ff3ab99",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *How can computing innovations which make use of Machine Learning reflect existing human bias?*\n- *How could it be used to discriminate against groups of individuals?*\n- *How can that bias be minimized?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that machine learning can reflect human bias because people choose the training data and write the algorithms, and that collecting more data does not remove bias. They should begin to suggest ways to reduce it, such as more diverse training examples."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "efd5752d-a344-46f2-b724-2a915ff3ab99",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fca6c6b7-d233-4989-aacc-f6796e6621c3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c6ffb580-fa82-43cb-964f-45d9f7911acc",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to Lesson 2, Level 6 to answer the checking for understanding question.",
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use this level to check whether students can explain how training data can lead to unfair results, recognize that the app learns from examples instead of following rules, and describe a step designers can take to reduce bias before launch."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c6ffb580-fa82-43cb-964f-45d9f7911acc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fca6c6b7-d233-4989-aacc-f6796e6621c3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c0eea714-882d-445e-b679-9f458526f22b",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c0eea714-882d-445e-b679-9f458526f22b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fca6c6b7-d233-4989-aacc-f6796e6621c3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7780b3e0-148e-4365-ae04-1bb223f637a0",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7780b3e0-148e-4365-ae04-1bb223f637a0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f3c5e26b-5583-4974-944d-1efb01b4e1ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5ef8af83-8d8f-4d0c-9f4e-4af8f5c7db5f",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to just think about what they learned today."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5ef8af83-8d8f-4d0c-9f4e-4af8f5c7db5f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f3c5e26b-5583-4974-944d-1efb01b4e1ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3de3dd15-24a8-46a8-bd81-e4df16c132fa",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *A spam filter deletes any email that contains the word \"free.\" What is the model in this AI system, and is it rules-based or learned? How do you know?*\n- *How would the filter change if it learned from emails that people marked as spam?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that the model is the part of the filter that decides which emails to delete, and that the first filter is rules-based because people wrote the rule. Students should recognize that the second filter is learned because it finds patterns in examples, and that each kind fails in its own way: the rule misses messages nobody wrote a rule for, while the learned filter depends on which examples it was given.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students say the learned filter is simply better, ask, \"What if the emails people marked as spam were incomplete or unfair?\" This connects back to training data and bias from the activity."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3de3dd15-24a8-46a8-bd81-e4df16c132fa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f3c5e26b-5583-4974-944d-1efb01b4e1ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ae9bdb5c-2871-42a0-852b-d91f71dc1613",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ae9bdb5c-2871-42a0-852b-d91f71dc1613",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f3c5e26b-5583-4974-944d-1efb01b4e1ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4f80c2b8-956d-4c6a-b46b-7d98100854e5",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Teachers and students should complete the end of lesson survey.",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4f80c2b8-956d-4c6a-b46b-7d98100854e5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f3c5e26b-5583-4974-944d-1efb01b4e1ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "811830bf-dbbf-4b1b-a4b0-d4fab3474c24",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students discover next word prediction by doing it themselves, before generative AI or LLMs are named.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each group a set of sentence starter cards.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students in pairs or small groups. One student draws a sentence starter card and reads it aloud (e.g. \"The weather today is...\"). Without discussing, everyone else silently writes the word they think comes next."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "811830bf-dbbf-4b1b-a4b0-d4fab3474c24",
        "lesson_activity.key": "921f18cf-3449-4789-bf02-bda099c0776c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cd7951e9-354f-4b90-8a74-4bb8b022b071",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *What word did each person write?*\n- *Where did you agree? Where did you disagree*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students notice agreement on predictable starters and disagreement on ambiguous ones. Some words are simply more likely to come next than others.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students describe their guesses as \"obvious\" or \"random,\" that is the vocabulary you want. Reuse \"likely\" and \"pattern\" when you introduce generative AI next."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cd7951e9-354f-4b90-8a74-4bb8b022b071",
        "lesson_activity.key": "921f18cf-3449-4789-bf02-bda099c0776c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "656e1795-eedf-457e-8ff2-ec59873dfcd0",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "656e1795-eedf-457e-8ff2-ec59873dfcd0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "921f18cf-3449-4789-bf02-bda099c0776c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fd849710-84a8-46a3-8cfa-ed4f0b102e70",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fd849710-84a8-46a3-8cfa-ed4f0b102e70",
