    },
    {
      "key": "b9f78746-27a2-4ab7-904a-2677f25cd990",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students discover, through voting on real messages, that a confidence score is not the same as being correct — and that catching what the bot misses takes a human who actually reads for tone and context. This is exactly how AI moderation fails in the real world: missing slang it was never trained on.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the scenario. Explain that students are acting as admins for a large gaming Discord community whose AI moderation bot has flagged four messages, each with a toxicity score and a suggested action. Clarify that the toxicity score is how confident the bot is, not a fact about the message — a high score just means the bot is sure, not that it's right."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b9f78746-27a2-4ab7-904a-2677f25cd990",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b4596c44-3bbf-4543-83f3-23a28041bdb8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8c8abe6c-8823-4529-9dc5-bbddacef267a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the three-part checklist. Tell students that before deciding on any flagged message, they run it through Tone, Context, and Stakes.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Tone might be sarcasm read as an insult, Context might be a long-time member versus a brand-new account, and Stakes might be a joke that could still get someone temporarily banned."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8c8abe6c-8823-4529-9dc5-bbddacef267a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b4596c44-3bbf-4543-83f3-23a28041bdb8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "66c0559c-e386-4c62-a11e-bf81c6f8452e",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** For each message, students vote with a hand signal: thumbs up to approve the bot's action, thumbs down to override it, or sideways to escalate to a human moderator."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "66c0559c-e386-4c62-a11e-bf81c6f8452e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b4596c44-3bbf-4543-83f3-23a28041bdb8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a029d30d-f60e-4e5a-8e60-78e24c30481e",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Read the first message aloud, then have students vote by hand signal: approve, override, or escalate. Call on a couple students to share a reason before revealing the next message."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a029d30d-f60e-4e5a-8e60-78e24c30481e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b4596c44-3bbf-4543-83f3-23a28041bdb8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bcbb8077-de2f-4537-aea8-b6f9b7a6320b",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Read the second message aloud, then have students vote by hand signal: approve, override, or escalate. Call on a couple students to share a reason before revealing the next message."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bcbb8077-de2f-4537-aea8-b6f9b7a6320b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b4596c44-3bbf-4543-83f3-23a28041bdb8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8776fc84-408e-439c-9a0b-b143de6e3219",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Read the third message aloud, then have students vote by hand signal: approve, override, or escalate. Call on a couple students to share a reason before revealing the next message.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Watch for students overriding this one out of habit from the last two messages. If most of the class overrides it, pause here rather than waiting for the pattern slide, and ask what is actually different about this sender."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8776fc84-408e-439c-9a0b-b143de6e3219",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b4596c44-3bbf-4543-83f3-23a28041bdb8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5f409cbc-8245-4510-8438-38e699764cb3",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Read the final message aloud, then have students vote by hand signal: approve, override, or escalate. Call on a couple students to share a reason before moving to the discussion."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5f409cbc-8245-4510-8438-38e699764cb3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b4596c44-3bbf-4543-83f3-23a28041bdb8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "969ef12c-8f1c-422a-b92f-63f20b76cfec",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *What do you notice about the messages you overrode? Is there a pattern?*\n- *What does the bot actually need?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should notice that every override involved a veteran member using slang the bot was never trained on, while the one real problem came from a brand-new account — no need for the term \"model drift\" yet. On the fix, guide them to see that retraining alone or adjusting the model alone won't work; the bot needs new data that includes this community's slang, then needs to be retrained on it."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "969ef12c-8f1c-422a-b92f-63f20b76cfec",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b4596c44-3bbf-4543-83f3-23a28041bdb8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4b2a51ce-51c6-4565-92c7-8aec8a166a63",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4b2a51ce-51c6-4565-92c7-8aec8a166a63",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b4596c44-3bbf-4543-83f3-23a28041bdb8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5fee9742-fa9a-421b-b360-d7bcfb645be3",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5fee9742-fa9a-421b-b360-d7bcfb645be3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b4596c44-3bbf-4543-83f3-23a28041bdb8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d049267c-ec6f-4e62-a51d-41a8742a7c7d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students name what they just experienced in the warm-up — model drift — then build the vocabulary and toolkit (the four ways things can go wrong, the four possible actions) they'll need to diagnose and respond to events during today's simulation.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to take out their Unit 1 Unit Guide.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Build Your Toolkit"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d049267c-ec6f-4e62-a51d-41a8742a7c7d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d04a1ef8-76ae-45f9-a7c9-81feabae6db4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "58984422-7b31-4f53-9ef4-1e2dfbfda60b",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define model drift. Point back to the Auto-Mod bot from the warm-up — its accuracy on veteran members using new slang is drift in action. Have students record the definition in their Unit Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "58984422-7b31-4f53-9ef4-1e2dfbfda60b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d04a1ef8-76ae-45f9-a7c9-81feabae6db4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d490614c-29c3-4c19-97e1-15e6de454e36",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define each of the four categories. Tell students these are the four things they'll need to diagnose during today's activity.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Don't preview which action fixes each category — figuring that out themselves is the point of Keep It Running. This slide should name the four problems, not the four solutions."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d490614c-29c3-4c19-97e1-15e6de454e36",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d04a1ef8-76ae-45f9-a7c9-81feabae6db4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f14bb87f-647d-4cb2-988d-1f6e4d219ac3",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that a model can't catch its own drift. A human monitoring performance is what catches it, and a human decides how to respond.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Point back to two moments students already experienced: in the warm-up, the bot never noticed its own pattern of mistakes — a human (them) had to catch it. In \"Catching Drift Takes a Human,\" it's the same idea, just named as a standard: humans are the ones who monitor, and humans are the ones who decide what happens next."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f14bb87f-647d-4cb2-988d-1f6e4d219ac3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d04a1ef8-76ae-45f9-a7c9-81feabae6db4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1343424d-4fc7-4e95-8f99-fe0e92959b82",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *transparency statement*. Have students record the definition in their Unit Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1343424d-4fc7-4e95-8f99-fe0e92959b82",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d04a1ef8-76ae-45f9-a7c9-81feabae6db4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "222d6232-5dda-4fd6-94a2-5a8bea900b17",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Use the slide to tell students that an AI system at launch is not the same system a year later. Tell them they're about to spend a year finding out exactly what that means.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This is the moment to make explicit that students are about to be the human choice this slide describes — every action they take in the simulation is one of the differences between their system at launch and their system a year later."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "222d6232-5dda-4fd6-94a2-5a8bea900b17",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d04a1ef8-76ae-45f9-a7c9-81feabae6db4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f0853c7b-362a-46c6-b437-a58a2e1b958c",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the four actions students will choose between during today's simulation. Do not tell students which action fixes which type of problem — figuring that out is the point of the activity."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f0853c7b-362a-46c6-b437-a58a2e1b958c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d04a1ef8-76ae-45f9-a7c9-81feabae6db4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8e06184e-274d-4a56-b022-371005eb8adf",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students diagnose what's happening to their AI system and choose how to respond, then connect that experience back to the warm-up and to real AI systems they actually use during the debrief.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Pair students up before starting. Partners work through Level 1 together, discussing each event and deciding how to respond as a team.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Levels 1 to complete Level 1 with their partner. Students should complete Level 2 individually.\n- **Level 1:** Students go through four rounds of the simulation. Each round is one of the four ways things can go wrong.\n- **Level 2:** Students complete a check for understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for justifications that don't actually explain the reasoning (a single word, or a restatement of the action) — ask pairs to say their reasoning out loud before typing it. Also watch for pairs clicking \"Skip\" on a stale transparency statement field without noticing why it's flagged.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Keep It Running",
        "progression_name": "Keep It Running",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "An optional Activity Guide is available for Level 1. It isn't required — experiencing the simulation is meant to be a learning experience, not a formative assessment. If you'd like students to turn in evidence of their work, you can distribute the activity guide to students which gives them two options: screenshot the Year in Review summary screen, or copy and paste the summary text."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 2 checks all three lesson objectives. The Decision That Mattered checks that students can name a real human decision from their own gameplay and its effect — the core of the standard. Spot the Drift checks whether they can recognize model drift in a new scenario and explain how humans should respond. What Users Deserve to Know checks whether they can describe what a transparency statement must include and why it matters to users."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8e06184e-274d-4a56-b022-371005eb8adf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d04a1ef8-76ae-45f9-a7c9-81feabae6db4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dd99b5a9-58e3-430d-8b80-5af59fb0d88b",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *Where did you see the same kind of mistake happen in both the warm-up and the simulation — a system getting something wrong because the world around it changed, not because it was broken?*\n- *Where have you seen this happen in a real AI system you've actually used?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to name the shared thread explicitly: in the warm-up, the bot misread messages because gaming slang had shifted beyond what it was trained on; in the simulation, at least one event (drift or usage shift) put their system in the same position — right at launch, wrong once the world moved. The system in both cases wasn't \"broken,\" it just hadn't caught up. For the second question, listen for real examples like a recommendation algorithm missing a new trend, a spam filter missing new scam wording, or a voice assistant struggling with an accent or slang it wasn't trained on — the specific example matters less than whether students can correctly identify it as the same pattern: real-world change outpacing what a system was built to handle."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dd99b5a9-58e3-430d-8b80-5af59fb0d88b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d04a1ef8-76ae-45f9-a7c9-81feabae6db4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e6872d85-5ca2-4f24-b8d6-5eac2323a20a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e6872d85-5ca2-4f24-b8d6-5eac2323a20a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "97cc1f21-3dd3-41e9-bb47-a8af96ca2361"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fd51fae8-82e2-433f-8825-3c9688237787",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Give students thirty seconds to write one word on scrap paper, a sticky note, or the back of their Unit Guide that answers the Question of the Day. On your count of three, everyone holds their word up at once.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Scan the room as words go up. Call out three or four that capture the lesson well, and briefly address any that reveal a misunderstanding before moving on."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fd51fae8-82e2-433f-8825-3c9688237787",
        "lesson_activity.key": "97cc1f21-3dd3-41e9-bb47-a8af96ca2361"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d3030e29-3c6c-452e-99ea-ee34252da37c",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d3030e29-3c6c-452e-99ea-ee34252da37c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "97cc1f21-3dd3-41e9-bb47-a8af96ca2361"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b2bd9eb2-519f-4291-b66e-d5640943627b",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Teachers and students should complete the end of lesson survey.",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b2bd9eb2-519f-4291-b66e-d5640943627b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "97cc1f21-3dd3-41e9-bb47-a8af96ca2361"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "737a996b-575f-4754-ac1b-cab1ae3d66e8",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today we are going to try our own hands at cropping and then closely examine a real-world situation where training a model to crop photos leads to algorithmic bias.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "737a996b-575f-4754-ac1b-cab1ae3d66e8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "65188c43-6a42-434b-b816-8b68df8d6386"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "911eaacd-8af1-42b7-916d-31cd918bcf62",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- Think about a time when you shared a picture with your friends or on social media.\n- *Have you ever had to crop a photo you shared?*\n- *How did you decide what to crop out of the picture?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Use this discussion to show that users usually have a reason for sharing photos and the focus depends on that reason. Students will most likely bring up a wide range of examples that can be used to highlight how there isn’t a one size fits all approach to sharing photos and cropping them."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "911eaacd-8af1-42b7-916d-31cd918bcf62",
        "lesson_activity.key": "65188c43-6a42-434b-b816-8b68df8d6386"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "650bb175-c582-4c35-8db0-b4322258eda4",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "650bb175-c582-4c35-8db0-b4322258eda4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "65188c43-6a42-434b-b816-8b68df8d6386"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "631b63f4-818a-4700-89ca-17791e2c9130",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "631b63f4-818a-4700-89ca-17791e2c9130",
        "lesson_activity.key": "65188c43-6a42-434b-b816-8b68df8d6386"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "43007353-f896-4120-b38a-b1987b03baf3",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students crop images as if they were posting to social media, then compare choices with a partner to see that different people make different decisions about what to keep and what to leave out.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Level 1 to crop the images as if they were uploading them to a social media account. Students can also upload their own images and crop them as well.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Cropping Widget",
        "progression_name": "Cropping Widget"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "43007353-f896-4120-b38a-b1987b03baf3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7f6c5587-90a2-4e43-8802-7f28c73aee0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3421ad57-0acd-4270-a0ee-fe925f369941",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Which images were challenging for you to decide how to crop?*\n- *Which images did you and your partner crop the same?*\n- *Which images did you and your partner crop differently?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Use this discussion to highlight the different experiences and perspectives everyone brings to deciding how to crop an image. Direct students to consider not only what they decided to keep in the picture, but also what they decided to crop out of the picture. What happens when we crop something out of a picture? It never gets seen by the audience. Look back at the photos you cropped. What or who got left out? You may choose to guide the class toward describing general characteristics of images that were challenging to crop."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3421ad57-0acd-4270-a0ee-fe925f369941",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7f6c5587-90a2-4e43-8802-7f28c73aee0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6f809357-daa8-4038-b79d-a86d9d29a89c",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now imagine we recorded how everyone in this room cropped the images from this widget. We could use that information to train a model, just like we did with the fish earlier in the unit. This model could then be used to crop other photos being uploaded to the social media site.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6f809357-daa8-4038-b79d-a86d9d29a89c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7f6c5587-90a2-4e43-8802-7f28c73aee0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "81ba933b-9835-4b26-98b9-8daae41b5888",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How might the cropping data from our classroom be biased?*\n- *What are some ways we could address the biases in our data?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that if this were training a model, bias could arise from a few different places.\n- The limited set of pictures included in the app that are most likely not representative of the photos being shared on social media.\n- The choices of the students themselves. Are teenagers more likely to focus on certain things when cropping? Is this representative of the whole population of social media users?",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "When discussing bias, connect back to the training students did earlier in the unit when deciding what an ‘angry’ or ‘fun’ fish looks like, pushing students to consider how their opinions might lead to problems when training an AI model that would be widely used."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "81ba933b-9835-4b26-98b9-8daae41b5888",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7f6c5587-90a2-4e43-8802-7f28c73aee0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8151b88e-b768-4862-bbea-3844528a5cf4",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students watch a real case of a cropping algorithm that favored some people over others, then identify the evidence and the sources of bias.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Twitter Cropping Algorithm Video"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8151b88e-b768-4862-bbea-3844528a5cf4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7f6c5587-90a2-4e43-8802-7f28c73aee0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "462038d4-cc08-4edc-a2fa-0c0ed8c8c148",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "It turns out that the issue of how to crop an image is something social media platforms have been working on for some time. We are going to watch a video that shows the issues that arose when Twitter used machine learning to train an algorithm to do this cropping.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "462038d4-cc08-4edc-a2fa-0c0ed8c8c148",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7f6c5587-90a2-4e43-8802-7f28c73aee0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "50c12bb6-1238-4676-9d81-38bc917ab11d",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-video\"></i> **Video:** Show the \"Are We Automating Racism?\" video."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "50c12bb6-1238-4676-9d81-38bc917ab11d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7f6c5587-90a2-4e43-8802-7f28c73aee0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aad045a5-ea5b-4dee-bb8a-d8c0a295984b",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-video\"></i> **Video:** Show the second part of the video “Are We Automating Racism?”  (from 9:30 - 11:01)."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aad045a5-ea5b-4dee-bb8a-d8c0a295984b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7f6c5587-90a2-4e43-8802-7f28c73aee0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d7328f29-2ff1-49e4-a0e5-f03bf77e67cb",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Next, the reporter talks with an engineer who built a similar cropping tool, and then asks what data it was trained on.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d7328f29-2ff1-49e4-a0e5-f03bf77e67cb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7f6c5587-90a2-4e43-8802-7f28c73aee0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3934df41-a7a1-4a43-b7c6-3eed646fd011",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-video\"></i> **Video:** Show the the third part of the video “Are We Automating Racism?”  (from 12:52 - 13:46)."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3934df41-a7a1-4a43-b7c6-3eed646fd011",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7f6c5587-90a2-4e43-8802-7f28c73aee0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3bef2c74-07a9-4b62-9085-6782833be708",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *bias*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3bef2c74-07a9-4b62-9085-6782833be708",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7f6c5587-90a2-4e43-8802-7f28c73aee0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6c30492c-86a9-479c-8b08-78cfbedabf27",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *data* *bias*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6c30492c-86a9-479c-8b08-78cfbedabf27",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7f6c5587-90a2-4e43-8802-7f28c73aee0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7f0e60d6-0eb5-4b02-a491-b707bc4e851d",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *Who did the cropping algorithm favor, and what evidence in the video shows this?*\n- *Using our definition, is this bias? Why or why not?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that the reporters' tests and the tweets show the algorithm consistently kept lighter faces over darker faces, which fits the definition of bias. Students should recognize that the harm is about who gets seen, not just whether the crop was accurate.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Keep students focused on the evidence and on what the algorithm did, rather than on whether the machine itself is racist."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7f0e60d6-0eb5-4b02-a491-b707bc4e851d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7f6c5587-90a2-4e43-8802-7f28c73aee0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "626014cb-270a-4aea-8876-7a8f702aa444",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *How was the Twitter cropping algorithm trained?*\n- *According to the video, where is a potential source of bias when training similar cropping algorithms?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that the machine is not prejudiced. The bias came from design choices and data. Students should recognize that the model learned from saliency datasets built from photo libraries that do not represent the population as a whole (data bias), and that people chose that approach. Push students to explain how the two interact: choices about what data to collect shape how the app behaves for different users."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "626014cb-270a-4aea-8876-7a8f702aa444",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7f6c5587-90a2-4e43-8802-7f28c73aee0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "92c96d95-5200-466d-9b8e-4fd8ef729713",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students read and annotate an article to find where bias was identified, what Twitter tested, and what Twitter changed.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Twitter Cropping Algorithm article",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Twitter Cropping Algorithm Article"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "92c96d95-5200-466d-9b8e-4fd8ef729713",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7f6c5587-90a2-4e43-8802-7f28c73aee0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5098ddd5-8cba-4ce0-9688-d105c7d8dcb0",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now that we've seen how the problem was discovered, we are going to read how it played out and how Twitter responded when presented with evidence of bias in their cropping algorithm.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5098ddd5-8cba-4ce0-9688-d105c7d8dcb0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7f6c5587-90a2-4e43-8802-7f28c73aee0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "77a267b6-1bfa-4f0b-9189-4253ad3d9971",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students read the article \"Twitter says its image-cropping algorithm was biased, so it's ditching it (CNN).\" After they are finished reading, they should mark up the text with the following:\n- Mark: Where Twitter found bias, what Twitter tested, and what Twitter changed\n- At The End: Write a 10-word summary of the article",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "To Print or Not To Print? This lesson is written assuming that you have printed out the article and have it physically available for students to write on, even though it is also possible to have students interact with this text digitally. If students read the article digitally, it is most important that they still follow the active reading strategies outlined in this lesson - highlighting the text, writing in the margins, and summarizing. This may require some additional time & instruction to teach students your preferred tools of digital annotation, and may require some additional adjustments to some of the later annotation strategies in this lesson."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "77a267b6-1bfa-4f0b-9189-4253ad3d9971",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7f6c5587-90a2-4e43-8802-7f28c73aee0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "48ce8cb1-b761-43f4-a798-3c091b349582",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students show what they understand about how bias enters an app and when to automate or augment, then propose a specific revision to a biased design and describe how to retest it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Level 2 to complete Levels 2 through 4.\n- **Level 2:** Students identify how a human choice and a data choice can lead to an unfair result.\n- **Level 3:** Students decide how much an algorithm should be trusted with a high-stakes task.\n- **Level 4:** Students propose a revision to the cropping algorithm and describe how to retest it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As you walk around, listen for students tying their Level 4 change to a specific source of bias from the video, and for retests that include the people the algorithm treated unfairly. If students get stuck, ask which part of the algorithm they think caused the problem, and what photos they would run through the new version.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Check For Understanding",
        "progression_name": "Check For Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 2 to check whether students can connect a human choice about what data to collect to an unfair result for some users.\n\nUse Level 3 to check whether students can weigh speed and consistency against the stakes of a mistake, recognize when a task needs human judgment, and keep a person responsible for the final decision.\n\nUse Level 4 to check whether students can propose a specific revision tied to a source of bias, describe test photos that include the people affected, and state what result would show improvement."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "48ce8cb1-b761-43f4-a798-3c091b349582",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7f6c5587-90a2-4e43-8802-7f28c73aee0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1b3570d5-f5eb-4203-a242-3027277fb170",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1b3570d5-f5eb-4203-a242-3027277fb170",
        "lesson_activity.key": "73ed998e-05e7-47c2-b043-4cb5ef5e0f75"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cf312b58-05a6-4b31-bb87-fe8d548d0a18",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to just think about what they learned today."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cf312b58-05a6-4b31-bb87-fe8d548d0a18",
        "lesson_activity.key": "73ed998e-05e7-47c2-b043-4cb5ef5e0f75"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "009292e9-d22c-4661-8b78-fa5176a3c4b4",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "After testing, Twitter's team found bias in the cropping algorithm and largely stopped using it to crop photos. Here is one of the concerns they raised. Notice that they say the concern remains even if the algorithm treated every group equally. Take a moment to read it.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "009292e9-d22c-4661-8b78-fa5176a3c4b4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "73ed998e-05e7-47c2-b043-4cb5ef5e0f75"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "60910a14-fd56-488d-b4fc-b1c7e5da499b",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Read aloud, or instruct students to read, the response from Rumman Chowdhury on behalf of Twitter.\n\nSource: https://blog.twitter.com/engineering/en_us/topics/insights/2021/sharing-learnings-about-our-image-cropping-algorithm",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may not know \"representational harm.\" If they ask, explain that it is harm that comes from how people are shown, or whether they get to show themselves the way they want to be seen."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "60910a14-fd56-488d-b4fc-b1c7e5da499b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "73ed998e-05e7-47c2-b043-4cb5ef5e0f75"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2f001a48-324d-4b57-802f-1eba4ec870bb",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The story of Twitter and its photo cropping algorithm shows how something built with one intention, in this case to better highlight photos, can lead to unintended effects, in this case bias that favored lighter faces. It also shows a company taking action by establishing a machine learning ethics, transparency, and accountability team led by Rumman Chowdhury.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2f001a48-324d-4b57-802f-1eba4ec870bb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "73ed998e-05e7-47c2-b043-4cb5ef5e0f75"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ede42f2e-6393-45db-b100-20ac2d209f59",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- Do you think Twitter's response was appropriate?\n- What are the risks and potential harms of systems, such as social media platforms like Twitter, becoming too dependent on machine learning?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that Twitter weighed the speed and consistency of automated cropping against the risk of representational harm, and concluded that cropping depends on what each person wants to show. Students should recognize that tasks involving personal judgment about how people are represented are poor candidates for automation, even when automation is faster.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students say Twitter should simply have fixed the algorithm, point them back to the quote on the previous slide. Ask what concern would still remain even if the algorithm treated every group equally."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ede42f2e-6393-45db-b100-20ac2d209f59",
        "lesson_activity.key": "73ed998e-05e7-47c2-b043-4cb5ef5e0f75"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2c8af819-2b87-4471-8dfc-227ac4061169",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2c8af819-2b87-4471-8dfc-227ac4061169",
        "lesson_activity.key": "73ed998e-05e7-47c2-b043-4cb5ef5e0f75"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "82dbb534-ab6a-4d21-bae5-10269d0e3d83",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Teachers and students should complete the end of lesson survey.",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "82dbb534-ab6a-4d21-bae5-10269d0e3d83",
        "lesson_activity.key": "73ed998e-05e7-47c2-b043-4cb5ef5e0f75"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "56be2b7c-9cae-4a71-bda0-02479db0df74",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Hook students into today’s scenario and surface that “accurate” isn’t an obvious, fixed standard — it’s something people have to define, and different people might define it differently. This sets up why Step 1 (defining criteria) matters before any testing happens.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What would count as a “good” answer here — and who decides?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should start to notice that “good” isn’t obvious or universal — it might depend on who’s asking (a student wanting a fast answer vs. a librarian needing it to be exact), and that someone has to decide on a standard before anyone can even test whether the chatbot meets it. There’s no right answer expected here — just a couple of volunteer responses to get students thinking about criteria before they define their own later in the lesson.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Keep this to under 2 minutes — it’s a quick hook, not a full discussion. One or two student responses is enough before moving to the lesson objectives."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "56be2b7c-9cae-4a71-bda0-02479db0df74",
        "lesson_activity.key": "577fe4e7-d153-474d-ba8f-1e8a4befe473"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a9943257-343e-4106-9a02-07dd48c30bbc",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a9943257-343e-4106-9a02-07dd48c30bbc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "577fe4e7-d153-474d-ba8f-1e8a4befe473"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a56d2a20-f6af-407e-b4b8-6cba9cb03fab",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day. Let students know this question comes back at the end of the lesson, after they test an AI chatbot themselves.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "No need to discuss this now — just pose it and move on. Students don’t have enough information yet to answer it meaningfully; the point is to give them something to hold onto and revisit once they’ve done the testing."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a56d2a20-f6af-407e-b4b8-6cba9cb03fab",
        "lesson_activity.key": "577fe4e7-d153-474d-ba8f-1e8a4befe473"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "192b48f8-139b-4570-9f56-72ccefc0b1b4",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students define what “accurate” means for a real AI use case, then write test questions that put their own criteria to the test.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each group a copy of the AI Lab activity guide.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Design Test Criteria"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "192b48f8-139b-4570-9f56-72ccefc0b1b4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8ff99e51-1bc3-4505-862e-ccd43c8aa5db"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3d4974ec-41f5-4261-a381-f656ef38d07b",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to Step 1 on their activity guide. Have students work with their partner to decide what a correct answer needs to include, what would make an answer unacceptable, and write their team’s accuracy criteria — the standard they use to score the chatbot’s responses later in Step 3.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for criteria that are too vague to actually score against later, like “it should be good” or “it should make sense.” Push pairs toward something specific and checkable — does it need to match a fact exactly? Does it need to admit when it’s not sure? If a pair is stuck, ask: “If the chatbot gave a confident-sounding answer that was wrong, how would you even know?”",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If needed, provide an example or two, but don’t over-scaffold this step — it’s meant to be student-generated. A pair that writes weaker criteria now still gets a valid learning experience in Step 4, when they discover their criteria let an inaccurate answer slip through as a “Meets.”"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3d4974ec-41f5-4261-a381-f656ef38d07b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8ff99e51-1bc3-4505-862e-ccd43c8aa5db"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2fb32ae2-4ff8-4874-9dac-510ffa7ccc70",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to Step 2 on their activity guide. Have them continue working with their partner and write six required test questions, making sure to cover all four categories listed — not just easy ones — plus up to two optional backup questions they aren’t required to test.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Make sure teams are covering all four categories, not just easy ones. Watch for pairs who write six questions that are all variations of the same type (e.g., all easy fact-lookups) — if you spot this, point them back to the four categories and ask which ones they haven’t tried yet."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2fb32ae2-4ff8-4874-9dac-510ffa7ccc70",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8ff99e51-1bc3-4505-862e-ccd43c8aa5db"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "898d1f4e-1a62-4927-a0e1-989a97d5c452",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students test their own questions against the chatbot, score each response using their own criteria, then compare results with their partner to find a pattern in what breaks the system.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 7, Level 1, to complete Steps 3 and 4 in their activity guide. Partners split their six questions and each test and score their own three on their own page in Step 3. In Step 4, partners bring their pages back together to compare results and identify a pattern.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for pairs scoring responses based on how confident or polished the answer sounds, rather than checking it against the Quick Reference sheet or their own Step 1 criteria. If a team marks every response “Meets,” ask them to point to the specific line on the reference sheet that confirms it. Also watch for teams rushing past Step 4 without actually comparing both partners’ pages side by side.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Test, Score, and Reflect",
        "progression_name": "Test, Score, and Reflect",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Steps 3–4 on the activity guide can be used as a formative check of the lesson objectives (specifically Skill 1.C / LO 1.3.B). Look for scoring that references specific evidence (the reference sheet or their own criteria) rather than general impressions, and a Step 4 pattern that correctly identifies where the chatbot lacked information rather than where it simply sounded off."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "898d1f4e-1a62-4927-a0e1-989a97d5c452",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8ff99e51-1bc3-4505-862e-ccd43c8aa5db"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9996d200-5884-49b9-b54e-95dfd19890a7",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 7, Levels 2-4.\n- **Level 2:** Students reflect on whether the library chatbot is accurate enough to trust without human review, using evidence from their own testing.\n- **Level 3:** Students think about who would be affected if a patron had relied on the chatbot’s holiday-hours mistake.\n- **Level 4:** Students apply that same judgment to a new scenario — a parks department chatbot — and explain their reasoning.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** On Level 2, watch for students giving a blanket yes/no without citing a specific example from their own testing — prompt them to point to a response they actually scored. On Level 3, watch for vague answers like “people could be confused” — push students toward a specific, plausible person or consequence tied to the actual scenario. On Level 4, watch for students who answer without explaining why this situation differs from the ones that worked fine during testing, or who don’t connect their answer back to anything they noticed with the library chatbot.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Check for Understanding",
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Levels 2–4 serve as a formative check of today’s lesson objectives. Level 2 shows whether students can judge accuracy using their own evidence rather than general impressions. Level 3 shows whether students can name a concrete, plausible consequence rather than a vague one. Level 4 shows whether that judgment transfers to a new situation — look for students who recognize the live, individual data problem (the permit application) as the same kind of gap as the library chatbot’s holiday blind spot, rather than giving a disconnected answer."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9996d200-5884-49b9-b54e-95dfd19890a7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8ff99e51-1bc3-4505-862e-ccd43c8aa5db"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ba148849-85b1-402d-917f-43f213b1884d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ba148849-85b1-402d-917f-43f213b1884d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fc4b4692-fe81-4aee-9583-dd120e966292"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "83778c5c-d7c1-43f0-a964-6b9d2fc5e888",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to think about what they learned today. Facilitate a whole-class discussion — invite a few students to share the pattern their team found in Step 4, and a few others to share their answer from Level 3 about who would be affected. Tie the discussion back to today’s lesson objectives. Listen for students connecting specific chatbot behavior to real consequences, not just general statements about AI being risky."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "83778c5c-d7c1-43f0-a964-6b9d2fc5e888",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fc4b4692-fe81-4aee-9583-dd120e966292"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b84d4765-0374-4981-96e7-40374fbbaf4d",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Teachers and students should complete the end of lesson survey.",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b84d4765-0374-4981-96e7-40374fbbaf4d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fc4b4692-fe81-4aee-9583-dd120e966292"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3baa0aac-67a4-4478-811d-710e2787665d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today we're going to look at what it takes to write \"good\" instructions. We all use instructions all the time, whether it's directions to get somewhere, instructions to fill out a form, or even instructions from teachers. To help us get started, let's brainstorm what \"bad\" instructions\" look like.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3baa0aac-67a4-4478-811d-710e2787665d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9cb4fb10-da4b-4aab-a8a5-3b121d66a7b2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "16e5c7a4-cf27-42de-9bf5-17c1be032f31",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Write down three different reasons you would call a set of instructions \"bad\". Be ready to share with a neighbor.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Aim to hear a few different students share reasons that instructions are \"bad\". The point here is just to get students thinking and there's no specific answer you're driving towards. Some possible ideas, however, might include:\n- Instructions are not clear on what to do\n- Instructions use confusing words\n- Instructions don't actually accomplish what they're supposed to",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Use the Retrieve-Pair-Share strategy to facilitate this discussion by directing students to write down their thoughts and ideas first, then pair up students to compare their answers and expand their notes. This allows time for individual processing and idea generation before sharing thoughts with others. Ask students to share the ideas they discussed with their partners as a class."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "16e5c7a4-cf27-42de-9bf5-17c1be032f31",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9cb4fb10-da4b-4aab-a8a5-3b121d66a7b2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "02832192-bee9-4a1b-84cf-cfcf143a2c81",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "02832192-bee9-4a1b-84cf-cfcf143a2c81",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9cb4fb10-da4b-4aab-a8a5-3b121d66a7b2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9eef4fc0-695b-44d9-bb7f-112704a9902d",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9eef4fc0-695b-44d9-bb7f-112704a9902d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9cb4fb10-da4b-4aab-a8a5-3b121d66a7b2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d4789271-a0d7-41cc-a27e-342dcd6b4ce7",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students write text-only instructions for a block design, then try to rebuild another group's design from their instructions alone, so they experience firsthand how ambiguous everyday language can be.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Place students in groups of two, optionally forming a group of three.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each group a small set of blocks (either LEGO or paper cutouts). Also have each group use a blank sheet of paper or a blank page in a journal.",
        "duration": "30",
        "name": "Lego Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This lesson is students' first introduction to the concept of a programming language. It helps them understand why programming languages exist by giving them a first-hand experience with the problems that programming languages are designed to address. Natural language is usually too ambiguous for giving precise instructions that can be followed correctly 100% of the time. We need to create more structured and precise programming languages in order to accomplish this."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d4789271-a0d7-41cc-a27e-342dcd6b4ce7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "65d02bda-495a-4fde-8e92-5f46a3d103cf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b9912e44-f010-46a1-ae22-585ec9d75af5",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students a couple minutes for Step 1. They will use their lego pieces to create their own design. Keep this quick since the bigger focus should be on the instructions."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b9912e44-f010-46a1-ae22-585ec9d75af5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "65d02bda-495a-4fde-8e92-5f46a3d103cf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "53b485c9-ce5f-4b41-80c0-e7ef353249dd",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students move to Step 2. Either have students sketch their design or take a picture. Make sure the image is on a separate piece of paper or the back side of a sheet of paper."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "53b485c9-ce5f-4b41-80c0-e7ef353249dd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "65d02bda-495a-4fde-8e92-5f46a3d103cf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b2c19298-8699-4c9f-a57c-3cf132b06162",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students move to Step 3. This is the most important step and should be given about 5 minutes. Encourage students to be as clear as possible while only using words. Encourage them to think about their brainstorm of \"bad\" instructions at the start of class to see if it can inspire them to make \"good\" ones."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b2c19298-8699-4c9f-a57c-3cf132b06162",
        "lesson_activity.key": "65d02bda-495a-4fde-8e92-5f46a3d103cf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e5909cc2-f089-4236-880a-e4e66a01db7b",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Move students to Step 4. Have students take apart their design, and then trade instructions with another group. Make sure they keep their recording of the design hidden."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e5909cc2-f089-4236-880a-e4e66a01db7b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "65d02bda-495a-4fde-8e92-5f46a3d103cf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "48ae2e5a-d0d8-482d-b5c1-ba84507b9b08",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students move to Step 5. Give students 3 minutes to follow the instructions from another group."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "48ae2e5a-d0d8-482d-b5c1-ba84507b9b08",
        "lesson_activity.key": "65d02bda-495a-4fde-8e92-5f46a3d103cf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "87182875-4c9f-4ee2-b789-f673bda93c79",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students 2 minutes for Step 6 where they compare what they built with the picture of the actual target. In their groups they should discuss what they think went wrong or anything they're surprised worked out."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "87182875-4c9f-4ee2-b789-f673bda93c79",
        "lesson_activity.key": "65d02bda-495a-4fde-8e92-5f46a3d103cf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "09d63cf9-78d5-4e0d-984a-862e3652a632",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Depending on how much time you have, encourage students to trade with one or two other groups. Make sure to save time for the wrap up.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "**Make it a Gallery Walk:** Depending on your room set-up you may be able to have students run this activity in a more open-ended way. Students should leave their unconstructed blocks and instructions at their table with the target image face down. They can then make their way to a few different groups' instructions over the course of 10-15 minutes.\n\n**Mark What's Confusing:** You may optionally have students leave suggestions on instructions when they use them explaining where they were confused or wanted more detail. This is a good way to practice giving and receiving feedback. It also forces groups to think more clearly about what is causing their confusion since they'll have to leave it in writing."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "09d63cf9-78d5-4e0d-984a-862e3652a632",
        "lesson_activity.key": "65d02bda-495a-4fde-8e92-5f46a3d103cf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b05ec7eb-820d-4893-85ab-74707a47dc64",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students show what they understand about natural and programming languages by describing how they differ and explaining why programming languages exist.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Unit 1 Lesson 8, Level 1 to complete the Checking for Understanding question.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Check Your Understanding",
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use these levels to check whether students can describe how natural and programming languages differ, connect ambiguity to the problems they saw in the activity, and justify why programming languages exist."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b05ec7eb-820d-4893-85ab-74707a47dc64",
        "lesson_activity.key": "65d02bda-495a-4fde-8e92-5f46a3d103cf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4081dfb9-1f39-40a1-9b86-ee863a5afee7",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4081dfb9-1f39-40a1-9b86-ee863a5afee7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aec65d36-7a0b-43b7-a66d-d731c3799744"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "adb0ba2e-af48-40e4-ae82-30652d319f87",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to just think about what they learned today."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "adb0ba2e-af48-40e4-ae82-30652d319f87",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aec65d36-7a0b-43b7-a66d-d731c3799744"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "853b0680-c1c9-4cc9-9446-0839128f8828",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** When you or your classmates made mistakes following instructions today what \"went wrong\"? Try to be as specific as possible.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that mistakes came from specific moments when an instruction could be read in more than one way. Students might say things like \"if we'd just been more clear\" or \"we just need to include more detail.\" This might be true, but eventually you should point out that human language is often ambiguous. In other words, it's not their fault! It's hard to do a good job with bad tools!"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "853b0680-c1c9-4cc9-9446-0839128f8828",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aec65d36-7a0b-43b7-a66d-d731c3799744"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8ab709cc-5cd0-4bbd-ba46-fed26c698db1",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Even trying to be as clear as possible we struggled to write clear instructions. There are things we could do to improve, but the core challenge here is that everyday human language is bad for giving clear instructions. Words can have two or three meanings! Instructions that seem clear turn out to be vague when we actually go follow them. If we want to give clear instructions, we need to fundamentally change the language we use. We need to create a new kind of language.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8ab709cc-5cd0-4bbd-ba46-fed26c698db1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aec65d36-7a0b-43b7-a66d-d731c3799744"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "af6609e1-fdd4-4dc5-ad4b-2ec36da237be",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Imagine we were going to redesign human language to be really good for giving clear instructions. What types of changes would we need to make?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that a language built for giving instructions would need to remove the ambiguity they ran into during the activity. Specific answers aren't important, but push students to build on their experiences in the main activity. For example, if they saw that words having two meanings was confusing, they should suggest redesigning the language so each word has a single well-understood meaning."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "af6609e1-fdd4-4dc5-ad4b-2ec36da237be",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aec65d36-7a0b-43b7-a66d-d731c3799744"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f01d4b41-bbbc-499e-86b4-f61ef38c74b4",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Teachers and students should complete the end of lesson survey.",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f01d4b41-bbbc-499e-86b4-f61ef38c74b4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aec65d36-7a0b-43b7-a66d-d731c3799744"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a423ec3a-9c0a-4d1d-ae71-b4826b6ae63c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the slide with the four problems students will need to solve.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Give students one minute to review the problems, then let them move around the room collecting the information they need. This should take about three minutes."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a423ec3a-9c0a-4d1d-ae71-b4826b6ae63c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c70e150a-41d2-495e-aeb3-7c1181c93f2e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "22fbb8a4-9e88-4ff1-bb46-1af455da99c4",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As computer scientists, it's important to ask “have I seen this problem before?” or “how is this problem similar to others I've solved?”",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "22fbb8a4-9e88-4ff1-bb46-1af455da99c4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c70e150a-41d2-495e-aeb3-7c1181c93f2e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "961a861a-8e59-44b1-9500-f42e6aa12a2d",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Which problems did you solve in a similar way?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that Problems 1 and 2 are solved the same way: as soon as you find one person who meets the criteria, you're done. Students should recognize that Problems 3 and 4 are solved the same way too: you have to ask every classmate and keep track of the closest birthday you've heard so far.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Have students share with a partner first, then discuss with the whole room."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "961a861a-8e59-44b1-9500-f42e6aa12a2d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c70e150a-41d2-495e-aeb3-7c1181c93f2e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4949b2fa-b95e-465b-849e-196e7fa4c8fc",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4949b2fa-b95e-465b-849e-196e7fa4c8fc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c70e150a-41d2-495e-aeb3-7c1181c93f2e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8bc7c62a-6b3a-4204-a8ad-6e7b5d3d8d43",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8bc7c62a-6b3a-4204-a8ad-6e7b5d3d8d43",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c70e150a-41d2-495e-aeb3-7c1181c93f2e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "68cbf1fc-a276-4ea7-a0d7-3febce6803a0",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *problem*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "68cbf1fc-a276-4ea7-a0d7-3febce6803a0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b991e9fa-bd22-4b12-a328-7158cdece18d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a684a744-94bd-4974-b4f4-686919b5ce83",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students trace pairs of flowcharts, write out the steps, and decide whether each pair solves the same problem.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Comparing Algorithms"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a684a744-94bd-4974-b4f4-686919b5ce83",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b991e9fa-bd22-4b12-a328-7158cdece18d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "93da676e-da36-4c89-b347-a696d8cbbd59",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We just thought about whether problems are similar. Now we're going to look at whether two sets of instructions are solving the same problem.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "93da676e-da36-4c89-b347-a696d8cbbd59",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b991e9fa-bd22-4b12-a328-7158cdece18d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "27c31f74-67e5-4a4b-91aa-2c76b18a9daa",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each pair of partners one activity guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "27c31f74-67e5-4a4b-91aa-2c76b18a9daa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b991e9fa-bd22-4b12-a328-7158cdece18d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f13bb14a-dcb5-4f01-bca8-7adceab2f5de",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In this activity, a robot follows instructions on a grid. It draws a line, called a trail, on every square it moves through. The robot starts facing north, toward the top of the grid. Left and right always mean the robot's left and right, not yours. Each flowchart is a set of instructions. You start at Start, follow the arrows one box at a time, and stop at End.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f13bb14a-dcb5-4f01-bca8-7adceab2f5de",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b991e9fa-bd22-4b12-a328-7158cdece18d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6d2d60d1-aa01-458e-b3e6-26ced3b46a94",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students often turn relative to the page instead of the robot. Have them turn their bodies, or rotate their paper, to match the robot's facing before they draw."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6d2d60d1-aa01-458e-b3e6-26ced3b46a94",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b991e9fa-bd22-4b12-a328-7158cdece18d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9e4e3645-b095-499e-8bc9-8fce67749534",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let's trace Flowchart A together. Turn right, so the robot faces east. Move forward, so it draws one segment east. Let's write that as steps: one, turn right. Two, move forward. Now Flowchart B. Turn left three times: north to west, west to south, south to east. Move forward, one segment east. Same trail. The flowcharts look different, but they solve the same problem.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9e4e3645-b095-499e-8bc9-8fce67749534",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b991e9fa-bd22-4b12-a328-7158cdece18d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f6d7d64b-57e3-417b-90ab-d0371d0921da",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click the animated slide to reveal the answer: Same."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f6d7d64b-57e3-417b-90ab-d0371d0921da",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b991e9fa-bd22-4b12-a328-7158cdece18d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a7759a3c-f3ed-4b9c-83e0-8134bcc45a8b",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *algorithm*. Refer back to the two flowcharts from the previous slide as examples of algorithms. To help students get a better understanding of what an algorithm is, make the connection to everyday tasks such as the steps involved in making a sandwich or tying your shoes as algorithms they use."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a7759a3c-f3ed-4b9c-83e0-8134bcc45a8b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b991e9fa-bd22-4b12-a328-7158cdece18d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ccb6a312-3414-4621-818c-c508226ea620",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *sequencing*. Use the two flowcharts in the activity guide as examples of sequencing."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ccb6a312-3414-4621-818c-c508226ea620",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b991e9fa-bd22-4b12-a328-7158cdece18d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "15ab5b7d-ffcd-4b35-b0fd-427555993c21",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have partners move to evaluate Paris 2 and 3. Partners should split each pair, one tracing Flowchart A and one tracing Flowchart B, then compare and decide together.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students turn from the robot's point of view, write one step per box, and use their trails as evidence before deciding.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use this activity to check whether students can trace a flowchart, write its steps in order, and use the result to justify whether two flowcharts are the same."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "15ab5b7d-ffcd-4b35-b0fd-427555993c21",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b991e9fa-bd22-4b12-a328-7158cdece18d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "45011140-765b-4943-8ccb-4371cae26de2",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click the animated slide to reveal the answer: Not the same. Both draw an L, but one bends right and the other bends left.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students who judge by shape may call these the same. Have them draw both trails on the same grid."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "45011140-765b-4943-8ccb-4371cae26de2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b991e9fa-bd22-4b12-a328-7158cdece18d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "92bf0048-96c8-457e-a04c-5f59a0ea9c13",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click the animated slide to reveal the answer: Same. Two right turns and two left turns both leave the robot facing south, so both draw one segment south."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "92bf0048-96c8-457e-a04c-5f59a0ea9c13",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b991e9fa-bd22-4b12-a328-7158cdece18d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cde3eb81-f836-4313-9ac7-f69ec88e494a",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let's look at this one together. Flowchart B has a diamond, which asks a question. If the answer is no, the arrow sends the robot back to move again. If yes, it goes to End. Follow the arrows: the robot moves four times, then stops.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cde3eb81-f836-4313-9ac7-f69ec88e494a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b991e9fa-bd22-4b12-a328-7158cdece18d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6fc4c021-6985-49cb-a9bd-f565bd082a61",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click the animated slide to reveal the answer: Same. Both draw a line four squares long.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "You don't need to teach how loops work. Students only need to follow the arrows."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6fc4c021-6985-49cb-a9bd-f565bd082a61",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b991e9fa-bd22-4b12-a328-7158cdece18d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0a0c8000-e88d-4017-bd64-e1579fa4b89d",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *What did the “same” pairs have in common that the “not the same” pair did not?*\n- *What would you say makes two sets of instructions “the same”?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that the “same” pairs drew the same trail even though the flowcharts looked different, with different numbers of boxes, different turns, or a diamond. Students should recognize that Pair 2 looked almost identical but drew different trails. Students should recognize that two sets of instructions are the same when they accomplish the same task, not when they look alike."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0a0c8000-e88d-4017-bd64-e1579fa4b89d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b991e9fa-bd22-4b12-a328-7158cdece18d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0a26af5d-5b9f-4403-b8fa-8292ad0b163c",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Check whether students can explain why two algorithms can look different but solve the same problem, define a problem, an algorithm, and sequencing, and match written steps to a flowchart.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Unit 1 Lesson 9, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 3.\n- **Level 1:** Students look at a new pair of flowcharts and explain whether they solve the same problem, using the trail as evidence.\n- **Level 2:** Students explain the difference between a problem and an algorithm in their own words, and what sequencing means for an algorithm.\n- **Level 3:** Students choose which flowchart matches a set of written steps.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Check Your Understanding",
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use these levels to check whether students can explain why two algorithms can look different but solve the same problem, define a problem, an algorithm, and sequencing, and match written steps to a flowchart."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0a26af5d-5b9f-4403-b8fa-8292ad0b163c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b991e9fa-bd22-4b12-a328-7158cdece18d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8e18534c-6831-40e2-95a5-9eef4d06f2e3",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8e18534c-6831-40e2-95a5-9eef4d06f2e3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "11e749ef-38f3-48fd-a937-f53d7feaef9a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "042c2198-6df3-4d56-9735-3a4a4d8c5bd6",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to just think about what they learned today."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "042c2198-6df3-4d56-9735-3a4a4d8c5bd6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "11e749ef-38f3-48fd-a937-f53d7feaef9a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9701a2e5-8472-44b6-ac28-29ca6f925bd6",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Computer scientists don't just think about code, they think about problems and the algorithms that solve them. Next, you'll start writing instructions that a computer can run.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9701a2e5-8472-44b6-ac28-29ca6f925bd6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "11e749ef-38f3-48fd-a937-f53d7feaef9a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "92d584a9-1f8f-400c-ac44-50a33f7f1d26",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** How did today’s activities change the way you think about algorithms and problems?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that algorithms are separate from the problems they solve, and that the same problem can be solved in different ways.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Have students think on their own, share with a partner, then discuss with the whole room."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "92d584a9-1f8f-400c-ac44-50a33f7f1d26",
        "lesson_activity.key": "11e749ef-38f3-48fd-a937-f53d7feaef9a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fd768bcd-29e4-42e4-bc24-996e76102fe5",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fd768bcd-29e4-42e4-bc24-996e76102fe5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "11e749ef-38f3-48fd-a937-f53d7feaef9a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0a855fc4-2adf-464c-bc84-48ef4c81355a",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Teachers and students should complete the end of lesson survey.",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0a855fc4-2adf-464c-bc84-48ef4c81355a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "11e749ef-38f3-48fd-a937-f53d7feaef9a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6b89b66e-2eb2-4e5d-a9c1-3b5fc2d22a49",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students draw on what they discovered in the Need for Programming Language lesson — that natural language is ambiguous while programming languages need precise, unambiguous rules — to set up why exact syntax matters in today's coding activity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *List two to three differences between a natural language and a programming language.*\n- *Why do those differences need to exist?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should surface ideas like: programming languages have strict, limited syntax while natural language allows many ways to say the same thing; programming languages can't tolerate ambiguity because the computer will only do exactly what's written; natural language relies on context and shared understanding between people, which code can't assume. If students reference the Need for Programming Language lesson (e.g. the Lego activity) to support their answer, that's a strong sign the connection landed."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6b89b66e-2eb2-4e5d-a9c1-3b5fc2d22a49",
        "lesson_activity.key": "816eba17-1581-4be3-866c-1c69679c7f3b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b6f6cc9e-f8a0-4f62-8883-4fa73023f15d",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b6f6cc9e-f8a0-4f62-8883-4fa73023f15d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "816eba17-1581-4be3-866c-1c69679c7f3b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d03542b3-d023-4207-892e-99f06a4614e0",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d03542b3-d023-4207-892e-99f06a4614e0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "816eba17-1581-4be3-866c-1c69679c7f3b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "be2fb13a-24bc-4cd9-95f6-f7a38d616a8f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students explore the Neighborhood and the Painter, then predict and run their first program before anything is explained — the notice/wonder discussion and explainer slides that follow only formalize what students already discovered on their own.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce students to The Neighborhood. Point out that the grid is 8 spaces wide and 8 spaces tall, with coordinates starting at (0, 0) in the top-left.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Explore the Neighborhood",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Reinforce the idea that this is a 2D coordinate system, which they'll use to understand the Painter's movements."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "be2fb13a-24bc-4cd9-95f6-f7a38d616a8f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "964420a3-37cb-4875-ae00-121387a1127f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c431f354-f455-4971-98a7-2e46933960ed",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Point out obstacles like the traffic cone, food truck, and sidewalk. Explain that the **Painter** can't move on or through these obstacles."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c431f354-f455-4971-98a7-2e46933960ed",
        "lesson_activity.key": "964420a3-37cb-4875-ae00-121387a1127f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "96a85f0e-f937-42e4-aa94-99720d23d99f",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the **Painter** as the character students control with code. Emphasize that the **Painter** already has built-in abilities — move, turn, and paint. Students won't define how these actions work; instead, they'll learn how to tell the Painter what to do and when.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students ask how the Painter knows how to do these things, let them know these abilities are already built in — students will focus on combining and sequencing them, not building them from scratch.\n\nRefer back to the warm-up if needed: \"Just like natural language and programming language have different rules, giving the Painter instructions means being precise and exact — no ambiguity.\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "96a85f0e-f937-42e4-aa94-99720d23d99f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "964420a3-37cb-4875-ae00-121387a1127f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8b1aad52-28c1-4820-becd-c0006897c916",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the block party scenario. Explain that students will help the Painter mark up the street for the event today, and that they'll need to navigate around things already set up in the Neighborhood — like the food truck.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If any students aren't familiar with the term \"block party,\" a one-sentence explanation (a street closed off for a neighborhood celebration) is usually enough — the image should carry most of the meaning."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8b1aad52-28c1-4820-becd-c0006897c916",
        "lesson_activity.key": "964420a3-37cb-4875-ae00-121387a1127f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c6555270-889a-46c2-9919-d4ed33a0b5a6",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Place students into pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 1 to read a short program, predict what the Painter will do, choose their answer, then run the code to check.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Notice whether students are actually reading and tracing the code before choosing an answer, or just picking one quickly to unlock Run. If students seem to be guessing, ask them to walk through the code line by line and describe what each line does before locking in a choice.",
        "progression_name": "Predict and Run",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The CodeAI CSP curriculum uses several level types when introducing programming concepts. We do not recommend having students complete the Predict & Run or the Investigate & Modify levels in isolation. Instead, we encourage teachers to have students discuss and problem solve with a partner, for the class to pause and reflect as they work, and use the levels as opportunities to provide just-in-time feedback. Refer to the Guide to Programming Levels for teaching strategies and suggestions for supporting student learning while they engage with these levels."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c6555270-889a-46c2-9919-d4ed33a0b5a6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "964420a3-37cb-4875-ae00-121387a1127f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7120738f-c5fa-41a9-bf40-42166208924d",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *What did you notice about the code in this program?*\n- *What do you wonder about the code in this program?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students toward noticing the structural patterns in the code — that each command is written with words followed by parentheses (e.g., move(), turn_left()), that the commands run in order from top to bottom, and that the line starting with # is a comment, not something the Painter does. Students may also notice that only turn_left() appears and wonder whether a turn_right() command exists — encourage this wonder rather than answering it, since it sets up something they'll need to reason through as they work with the code."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7120738f-c5fa-41a9-bf40-42166208924d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "964420a3-37cb-4875-ae00-121387a1127f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0a87362b-37ed-41a8-95b0-611a9dc6134f",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the default conditions for every new Painter — at the top-left corner of the grid (0, 0), facing East, with 0 units of paint.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Clarify that \"faces East\" means moving right on the grid — this is often the first directional detail students mix up."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0a87362b-37ed-41a8-95b0-611a9dc6134f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "964420a3-37cb-4875-ae00-121387a1127f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b2626754-10e7-4b63-9ec2-ee6a34fed1c9",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to explain the import line. Emphasize that Python doesn't know what a Painter is unless you import it first."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b2626754-10e7-4b63-9ec2-ee6a34fed1c9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "964420a3-37cb-4875-ae00-121387a1127f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "399f4f5e-5e46-41ce-b017-fe43d1093327",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to explain **comments**. Point out that line 1 starts with **#** — ask students if they remember wondering about that line during the Notice/Wonder discussion. Emphasize that comments are ignored when the code runs; they're only there to help the reader."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "399f4f5e-5e46-41ce-b017-fe43d1093327",
        "lesson_activity.key": "964420a3-37cb-4875-ae00-121387a1127f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "841f001f-5988-4d1f-8ae0-252d9413a810",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students test and formalize what they noticed and wondered about in Predict & Run — tracing, tinkering with, and commenting the code themselves, then naming the vocabulary (programming statement, program, sequential programming) and new commands (take_paint(), paint()) they'll need for Modify.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 2 to trace, tinker, and comment on the code from Predict & Run.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for students who don't revert their turn_left() change back to three after tinkering — the instructions ask them to change it back when done. Check that predictions and comments (lines 8 and 14) reflect actual reasoning, not guesses, and listen for students connecting what they see in Tinker back to the \"no turn_right()\" wonder from the Notice/Wonder discussion.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Investigate",
        "progression_name": "Investigate"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "841f001f-5988-4d1f-8ae0-252d9413a810",
        "lesson_activity.key": "964420a3-37cb-4875-ae00-121387a1127f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f0512867-248b-4f86-adac-4f3a71642046",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to take out their copy of the CSP Unit Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f0512867-248b-4f86-adac-4f3a71642046",
        "lesson_activity.key": "964420a3-37cb-4875-ae00-121387a1127f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f6504b95-d6a4-4390-a5c3-582ce57b26e1",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *programming statement*. Point to the code shown — each line (move(), turn_left()) is a programming statement. Ask students what other \"statements\" they'd expect to see later in the course. Have students annotate the code snippet in their unit guide, circling or highlighting one line and labeling it as a programming statement."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f6504b95-d6a4-4390-a5c3-582ce57b26e1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "964420a3-37cb-4875-ae00-121387a1127f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3b377a8c-f353-4d63-8936-081c4b5f6858",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *program*. Point to the code shown — the whole set of five lines together is a program made up of individual programming statements. Ask students what happens if the statements run in a different order than they're written (connects to what they saw in Tinker). Have students annotate the code snippet in their unit guide, bracketing or highlighting all five lines and labeling them as a program."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3b377a8c-f353-4d63-8936-081c4b5f6858",
        "lesson_activity.key": "964420a3-37cb-4875-ae00-121387a1127f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "03a18ce4-55f9-45c7-af8c-e0291ba8760f",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *sequential programming*. Point to the code shown — the statements run top to bottom, one at a time, with no branching or user input. Ask students to imagine what would happen if computers could run statements in any order they wanted, rather than sequentially. Have students annotate the code snippet in their unit guide, drawing arrows or numbering the lines in the order they run."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "03a18ce4-55f9-45c7-af8c-e0291ba8760f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "964420a3-37cb-4875-ae00-121387a1127f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dc856a13-2eae-4895-98fa-166de7682e52",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to explain **set_paint()** and paint(). Emphasize that if the Painter runs out of paint, **paint()** won't paint — but the Painter will still follow the rest of the code and keep moving. Point out that a message will appear in the console letting them know there's no paint left."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dc856a13-2eae-4895-98fa-166de7682e52",
        "lesson_activity.key": "964420a3-37cb-4875-ae00-121387a1127f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8df7fddb-118a-4e5d-9a2e-ec881dc1f48a",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students practice applying and extending sequential code independently, then build debugging skills by tracing existing code to find and fix an error.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 3 to complete Levels 3 through 6.\n- **Level 3:** Students add paint commands to the existing code and extend it to finish the path.\n- **Level 4:** Students choose one of four scenarios and write code to finish painting the street to the cone.\n- **Level 5:** Students find and fix a bug in existing code.\n- **Level 6:** Students answer a check for understanding question.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for students trying to use a turn_right() command in Levels 3–5 — remind them only turn_left() exists and ask what combination of left turns gets the same result. In Level 4, some students may hesitate to commit to one scenario; reassure them any of the four options is fine and they don't need to try multiple. In Level 5, students who say the code \"doesn't work\" without pinpointing where should be prompted to trace it line by line, comparing each step to where the painter actually ends up. For students who finish early, ask them to try a different Level 4 scenario or explain their Level 5 fix to a partner.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Modify: Extend and Debug",
        "progression_name": "Modify: Extend and Debug",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 5 (Debug) and Level 6 (CFU) can both be used as formative assessments — Level 5 checks whether students can trace code to locate and correct an error, and Level 6 checks whether students can predict a painter's ending position and direction from a short sequence of commands."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8df7fddb-118a-4e5d-9a2e-ec881dc1f48a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "964420a3-37cb-4875-ae00-121387a1127f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0d8131e3-df9d-4762-892f-a372dbd7d1dd",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *What would happen if you ran your Level 3 or 4 code out of order — would the painter still end up in the same place?*\n- *Why did tracing the code line by line help you find the bug in Level 5?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to recognize that because code runs sequentially — one statement at a time, top to bottom — the order of commands directly determines the outcome, which is exactly why tracing line-by-line was an effective way to find the bug in Level 5. This ties directly back to the \"sequential programming\" vocabulary from earlier in the lesson and sets up why order will matter even more once conditionals and loops are introduced in Unit 2."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0d8131e3-df9d-4762-892f-a372dbd7d1dd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "964420a3-37cb-4875-ae00-121387a1127f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "05b12651-d8ae-4fe5-b8eb-6f3f419b47c0",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "05b12651-d8ae-4fe5-b8eb-6f3f419b47c0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a799c4e4-95e3-4263-9b87-2b070cd94ecf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e5829b36-4d4f-458b-87cd-09fbe9bab961",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students a moment to silently think about the Question of the Day and what they learned today. Let them know they'll use these reflections in the next activity."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e5829b36-4d4f-458b-87cd-09fbe9bab961",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a799c4e4-95e3-4263-9b87-2b070cd94ecf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "538935dd-98e6-4514-8088-0bd3f893abcd",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students turn to a partner and share their Glows, Grows, and Want-to-Knows from today's lesson, using their thinking from the Question of the Day to help shape their answers. Cold-call one or two pairs to share out with the class.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Glows, Grows, Want-to-Knows is a strategy to help students identify their strengths and areas of improvement and share any questions or misconceptions."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "538935dd-98e6-4514-8088-0bd3f893abcd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a799c4e4-95e3-4263-9b87-2b070cd94ecf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d7c05f39-93aa-4766-a926-fb14c82c6a4a",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d7c05f39-93aa-4766-a926-fb14c82c6a4a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a799c4e4-95e3-4263-9b87-2b070cd94ecf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "adb0c7f5-b298-4069-998b-43b36cbf6512",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Teachers and students should complete the end of lesson survey.",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "adb0c7f5-b298-4069-998b-43b36cbf6512",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a799c4e4-95e3-4263-9b87-2b070cd94ecf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "540d9bac-45a0-4e06-9e91-5bb244b4ec18",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students connect pixel art to real-world design by noticing how artists use a small number of squares, colors, and details to make an image recognizable.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Pixel artists do a lot with only a few squares. Can you identify one of the famous paintings in pixel art?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify artwork they recognize. The pixel art pieces depict (top to button, left to right): Girl with a Pearl Earring by Johannes Vermeer, Mona Lisa by Leonardo da Vinci, Sunflowers by Vincent van Gogh, Composition with Red, Blue and Yellow by Piet Mondrian, and The Starry Night by Vincent van Gogh."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "540d9bac-45a0-4e06-9e91-5bb244b4ec18",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2def49e2-a384-4c29-9ad4-ecc561179cd8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ce329342-d330-4792-885c-8a894a0a686f",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *What clues helped you figure them out?*\n- *What details did the artist keep?*\n- *What details did they leave out?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should notice that even simple pixel art involves intentional choices. Artists decide which colors, shapes, and key details are most important so the image is still recognizable, even when many details are removed."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ce329342-d330-4792-885c-8a894a0a686f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2def49e2-a384-4c29-9ad4-ecc561179cd8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "81b7f274-57d7-4340-8cca-bc1f24adcccd",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "81b7f274-57d7-4340-8cca-bc1f24adcccd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2def49e2-a384-4c29-9ad4-ecc561179cd8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6b817d71-c4b8-49c9-866b-2baab5f5df55",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6b817d71-c4b8-49c9-866b-2baab5f5df55",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2def49e2-a384-4c29-9ad4-ecc561179cd8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d90dc95f-dc85-4e40-add5-2745fe3a918d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students sketch and plan their pixel art before coding so they have a clear goal to work toward.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Design Your Program activity guide and set of color pencils.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Design Your Program"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d90dc95f-dc85-4e40-add5-2745fe3a918d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4302a6b6-0c4d-4e69-87bd-01bbbb90358d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "664ea669-c936-4662-8c26-73f951a139e8",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today you will create your own pixel art design, then use code to bring it to life. Before we code, we’re going to design. Your sketch is your plan. It should help you decide what colors you need, where your painter should move, and what parts of the image need to be painted",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "664ea669-c936-4662-8c26-73f951a139e8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4302a6b6-0c4d-4e69-87bd-01bbbb90358d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1c9837ba-f0eb-41dc-bcc6-1287b37e5e31",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students sketch their program design on the grid and answer the planning questions.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to start simple. A clear design with a few colors is better than a very detailed design that is hard to finish in time."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1c9837ba-f0eb-41dc-bcc6-1287b37e5e31",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4302a6b6-0c4d-4e69-87bd-01bbbb90358d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2ee34c00-4380-4660-9c35-e27e3bedb838",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to page 2 of the activity guide. Have them review the rubric before they begin coding."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2ee34c00-4380-4660-9c35-e27e3bedb838",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4302a6b6-0c4d-4e69-87bd-01bbbb90358d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f6fc1951-67c1-4a5b-8ecb-007ed1d823d5",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students use **set\\_paint()**, **move()**, **turn\\_left()**, and **paint()** to recreate their planned pixel art design.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 11, Level 1. Have students use their sketch as a guide while they code. Remind them to test often instead of waiting until the end.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students who are coding without checking their sketch, students who need help sequencing commands, and students who are stuck because their painter is facing the wrong direction.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Program Your Sketch",
        "progression_name": "Make: Program Your Sketch",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use this level to check whether students can write a sequential program and debug when the output does not match their intended design."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f6fc1951-67c1-4a5b-8ecb-007ed1d823d5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4302a6b6-0c4d-4e69-87bd-01bbbb90358d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f4eaa39f-ade9-4e5e-bdcb-e4c594f7cfed",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f4eaa39f-ade9-4e5e-bdcb-e4c594f7cfed",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96262caf-8c1b-4673-9b6f-a7e8759be630"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d61ca7bf-bd4e-4547-9752-63341a6aa6c1",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to just think about what they learned today."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d61ca7bf-bd4e-4547-9752-63341a6aa6c1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96262caf-8c1b-4673-9b6f-a7e8759be630"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2cc63b79-a0c4-4f82-abe7-2d9aa199801f",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students walk around or view shared projects. Ask them to notice both the artwork and the coding choices.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What do you notice? What do you wonder?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students see that there can be many different successful solutions, even when everyone uses the same basic commands."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2cc63b79-a0c4-4f82-abe7-2d9aa199801f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96262caf-8c1b-4673-9b6f-a7e8759be630"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6d0a21d0-5c48-4c5c-8ff2-0b3613b73000",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Teachers and students should complete the end of lesson survey.",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6d0a21d0-5c48-4c5c-8ff2-0b3613b73000",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96262caf-8c1b-4673-9b6f-a7e8759be630"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ac750a94-15da-4b45-9e51-bcf76db2d61c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students notice that apps store personally relevant information, and that some of it changes over time while other pieces stay fixed, without naming \"variables\" yet.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *What are some pieces of information a favorite app of yours might remember about you?*\n- *Which of those would stay the same every time you open it? Which might change?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students land on the fixed vs changing distinction themselves (a username probably stays the same, a high score changes) rather than being told it."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ac750a94-15da-4b45-9e51-bcf76db2d61c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "974eb27d-761f-4c85-8567-32dec50b9125"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2a986623-0875-4c49-86c3-eb5a4b32fa7f",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
