    {
      "key": "3eb486b4-fd7c-4a95-96e1-1a5f6fddf087",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3eb486b4-fd7c-4a95-96e1-1a5f6fddf087",
        "lesson_activity.key": "12cfa698-f8a7-4460-b0e0-a38e54108970"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "488c4133-99b9-4310-aad8-e4116399af1e",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students predict what the Spy Code Name program outputs before running it, using concatenation and substring syntax they haven't yet been taught by name.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 15, Level 1 to predict the output of a program that builds a \"code name\" by combining a substring of a pet name with a substring of a birth month, then run the program to check their prediction.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for how students reason about the unfamiliar bracket notation (**pet_name[0:3]**) — are they guessing randomly, or trying to infer a pattern from the numbers? Note any students who confidently name what they expect versus those who seem stuck, since this can help you group pairs for the Notice/Wonder discussion that follows.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Predict & Run",
        "progression_name": "Predict & Run"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "488c4133-99b9-4310-aad8-e4116399af1e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "12cfa698-f8a7-4460-b0e0-a38e54108970"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "38639ff5-8cf4-4904-8394-7e3dfbfdd0e6",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *What do you notice about the code in this program?*\n- *What do you wonder about the code in this program?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students describe in their own words what the code seemed to do — for example, that it grabbed a few letters from each variable and joined them together — without needing the terms \"concatenation\" or \"substring\" yet. Surface genuine wonderings (e.g., \"why 3 letters from one and 2 from the other?\") and let them stay open; these get addressed in Investigate, not answered here."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "38639ff5-8cf4-4904-8394-7e3dfbfdd0e6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "12cfa698-f8a7-4460-b0e0-a38e54108970"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "29e2a9b1-8370-4e69-880c-53c13c5ee293",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Have each student take out their a copy of the unit guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "29e2a9b1-8370-4e69-880c-53c13c5ee293",
        "lesson_activity.key": "12cfa698-f8a7-4460-b0e0-a38e54108970"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "30f131ad-44e5-41f3-8278-7b79884bbf40",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *string*. Remind students they've been using strings since the first Intro to Programming lesson with the Painter and the Neighborhood (e.g., paint(\"white\")) and again throughout the Variables lessons."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "30f131ad-44e5-41f3-8278-7b79884bbf40",
        "lesson_activity.key": "12cfa698-f8a7-4460-b0e0-a38e54108970"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9816c0ba-b1e1-41e7-aa49-e892cc3861dd",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *concatenation*. Point out both **+** symbols and how each one joins two pieces together.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Notice the two + symbols do different jobs — the first builds a new string and saves it in code_name; the second builds a string just for what gets printed. We'll dig into that difference more on the next slide."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9816c0ba-b1e1-41e7-aa49-e892cc3861dd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "12cfa698-f8a7-4460-b0e0-a38e54108970"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f873605b-29a0-4dcf-af4b-fa39b794853f",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide. Have students discuss with a partner what sticky they think lands in the greeting baggie, then reveal the answer (\"hi Ally!\"). If students don't predict correctly, use it to surface the two most common errors: forgetting the space inside \"hi \" (giving \"hiAlly!\"), and treating first as the literal text \"first\" instead of substituting its stored value.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If a pair predicts correctly right away, ask them what would happen if \"hi \" were written as \"hi\" (no space) — this previews the space-dropping error as a quick follow-up rather than only introducing it as a hypothetical if no one makes that mistake."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f873605b-29a0-4dcf-af4b-fa39b794853f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "12cfa698-f8a7-4460-b0e0-a38e54108970"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a4cd68d1-15c4-44f1-b322-0a9f68568e0b",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to reveal the first code example (lines 1–2). Give students a moment to discuss with a partner what they think the output will be before clicking to reveal it and the comma-spacing annotation. Point out that line 1 has two comma spots that don't look the same — \"I was born in \" already ends with a space, so the comma's automatic space stacks on top, creating a double space before July; \"and I am\" has no trailing space, so you only see the comma's single space before 16.  Repeat for the second example (lines 3–4): pause for a prediction before revealing the output and the concatenation annotation. Line 3's **+** doesn't add anything automatically at all — that's why \"hi \" needs its own trailing space, or the output would read \"hiAlly!\".",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students ask \"why not just use commas for everything?\" — the biggest reason is that comma-print only displays values once; it doesn't create anything you can save or reuse. Concatenation with + builds an actual string value you can store in a variable, like code_name in the Spy Code Name program, and use again later. As a smaller practical note: comma-print also adds spacing automatically (which can create surprises like a double space), while + never adds spacing on its own, so you're always in full control."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a4cd68d1-15c4-44f1-b322-0a9f68568e0b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "12cfa698-f8a7-4460-b0e0-a38e54108970"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ad4be66f-e134-471d-ae71-cd11be12e67b",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students test the existing code name formula against edge-case inputs — a short name, an empty string, and a single space — to discover how slicing behaves when there isn't much (or anything) to grab, without changing the formula itself.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 15, Level 2 to complete the investigate steps.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for whether students correctly distinguish Step 2's empty string from Step 3's single space — this is the most likely point of confusion. Also watch for students who expect Step 1 (short name) to throw an error; if they're surprised it doesn't, that's the exact discovery this activity is built around.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Investigate Strings",
        "progression_name": "Investigate",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 2 can be used as a formative check for the \"distinguish empty strings from strings with spaces\" objective — listen for whether students can articulate the Step 2 vs. Step 3 difference in their own words during Circulate, even though this level itself isn't a written check."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ad4be66f-e134-471d-ae71-cd11be12e67b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "12cfa698-f8a7-4460-b0e0-a38e54108970"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "86afca42-7dad-4710-aab3-e150fafc172b",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *substring*. Remind students this is the same thing they were just testing in Investigate — **pet_name[0:3]** grabbing part of \"Whiskers\" — now with a name attached. Give students a moment to discuss with a partner what they think **birth_month[0:2]** will grab, then reveal \"Oc\"."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "86afca42-7dad-4710-aab3-e150fafc172b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "12cfa698-f8a7-4460-b0e0-a38e54108970"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9d4d85c7-296e-4e12-9386-902cdba617f1",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show how **pet_name[0:3]** matches the numbers to the highlighted letters. Point out that 0 lands right before the W and 3 lands right after the i — not on a letter itself — which is why the slice stops at \"Whi\" instead of grabbing the s.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The numbers label the gaps between characters, not the characters themselves — this is the most common point of confusion with slicing. If a student expects index 3 to point at the 4th letter, use the diagram to show it sits just after the i (end of \"Whi\"), which is why **[0:3]** stops there instead of including the s."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9d4d85c7-296e-4e12-9386-902cdba617f1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "12cfa698-f8a7-4460-b0e0-a38e54108970"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "da237685-d024-4247-8a1b-d875f0afc56d",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students 2–3 minutes to write their predictions for all five slices in their Unit Guide, then have them compare answers with a partner before any are revealed. Cold-call students for their prediction on each item, one at a time, and after a few responses click to reveal that item's answer before moving to the next. For each reveal, ask a student to explain why the slice returned that result before moving on.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for whether students are just guessing versus reasoning through start/end positions using the \"How Substrings Work\" diagram from the previous slide. Item 4 (pet_name[2:2]) and item 5 (pet_name[1:100]) are the most likely to trip students up — watch for partners who disagree on these two specifically, since that's a good signal for who to cold-call on them.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Items 4 and 5 are the trickiest — pet_name[2:2] returns an empty string (not an error, since start and end are the same position), and pet_name[1:100] doesn't break even though 100 is far past the end of the string. If students are unsure, connect it back to Investigate: strings don't throw errors just because there isn't much (or anything) left to grab."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "da237685-d024-4247-8a1b-d875f0afc56d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "12cfa698-f8a7-4460-b0e0-a38e54108970"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "696caee3-2715-4b25-a8cb-046930c27914",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 3 to answer the check for understanding questions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** For Question 1, watch whether students trace the substring and concatenation separately or try to guess the output as a whole — if they're stuck, point them back to the \"How Substrings Work\" diagram rather than re-explaining it from scratch. For Question 2, listen for whether students' explanations name the actual distinction (an empty string has zero characters; a space is still one character) rather than just asserting the values are \"different\" without saying why.",
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Both questions on Level 3 serve as the formative check for this lesson's two objectives — Question 1 checks whether students can trace code that combines concatenation and substrings to determine the displayed output (1.6.C.4/.5), and Question 2 checks whether students can distinguish an empty string from a string containing a space and explain the difference in their own words (1.6.C.3)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "696caee3-2715-4b25-a8cb-046930c27914",
        "lesson_activity.key": "12cfa698-f8a7-4460-b0e0-a38e54108970"
      }
    },
