    {
      "key": "3c7c09a9-0bef-4653-9388-a1206ab85ea0",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students predict the output of code that mirrors the previous lesson's concatenation pattern, then discover that combining a string and a number causes an error — surfacing the need for a fix without yet naming str().\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What do you think line 4 will print?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students predict a printed value (likely something like \"Whiskers7\") based on Day 1's concatenation pattern, then discover the code actually breaks. Surface that concatenation worked fine when joining two strings, but something's different here — **lucky_number** isn't a string, it's a number. Name that this is the issue (mixing a string and a number with **+**), but don't formally introduce **str()** yet — that comes in the activity section.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This is a genuine surprise for most students, since nothing about the code looks obviously wrong — it follows the exact same pattern as **pet\\_name + birth\\_month** from Day 1. If students protest \"but it looks the same!\", that confusion is the point; it's what makes the fix land later in the activity."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3c7c09a9-0bef-4653-9388-a1206ab85ea0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "33ee303f-6109-46e2-af0c-96a03244a0b3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "44a7c758-8dfd-40d4-898b-a43842590041",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "44a7c758-8dfd-40d4-898b-a43842590041",
        "lesson_activity.key": "33ee303f-6109-46e2-af0c-96a03244a0b3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "64f30425-a728-4222-b036-bf0abc32c55f",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "64f30425-a728-4222-b036-bf0abc32c55f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "33ee303f-6109-46e2-af0c-96a03244a0b3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0761ed46-611f-4cc6-8560-5006dbfda596",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students learn what str() does and why it's needed to combine a number with strings, connecting back to the error they discovered in the warm-up.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Have each student get their copy of the Unit 1 Unit Guide",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Combining Strings and Numbers"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0761ed46-611f-4cc6-8560-5006dbfda596",
        "lesson_activity.key": "470f25f4-dfca-48bd-9482-479751c85ef1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "85a57271-ad7a-4301-a332-0d4847413051",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that str() converts a value into a string so it can be combined with text using +. Connect this back to the warm-up's error — **lucky\\_number** wasn't a string, so it couldn't be joined with **pet\\_name** until it's converted.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Some students may ask why Python doesn't just convert numbers automatically. It's worth naming that this is a deliberate design choice in many languages — combining different types silently can hide bugs, so Python asks programmers to be explicit about the conversion instead."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "85a57271-ad7a-4301-a332-0d4847413051",
        "lesson_activity.key": "470f25f4-dfca-48bd-9482-479751c85ef1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8a8766a1-0dbe-4143-a971-b0e6e2ea1d8e",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the annotated slide. Point out that **str()** doesn't change what's stored in **lucky_number** — it only creates a string version of the value for this specific line, so it can be combined with text. Ask students to predict what **print(code_name)** outputs now, then confirm it prints \"Whiskers7\"."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8a8766a1-0dbe-4143-a971-b0e6e2ea1d8e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "470f25f4-dfca-48bd-9482-479751c85ef1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0dbe3305-b2a9-4f0e-82c5-6dcc591de84c",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students apply **str()** alongside concatenation and substrings to modify an existing program, practice identifying and fixing bugs, then design, plan, build, test, and reflect on an original program using the same tools.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 16, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 3.\n- **Level 1:** Students modify the program they investigated in the previous lesson — changing which letters are extracted, adding a string ingredient, adding a numeric ingredient, and fixing the resulting error using **str()**.\n- **Level 2:** Students choose one program to debug, fix the bug, and run it to confirm the fix works.\n- **Level 3:** Students plan and build their own generator program using concatenation and substrings, test it with a classmate, and reflect on their program.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** During Modify, watch for whether students correctly identify the str() fix as the same error type from the warm-up. During Debug, listen for whether students can articulate why the code was broken, not just that they found a fix that works — especially on Option D, where the \"bug\" doesn't cause an error at all. During Make, check that students have a filled-out plan before coding, and that programs meet the technical requirements (at least one concatenation, one substring) before students move to testing.",
        "duration": "25",
        "name": "Modify and Make",
        "progression_name": "Modify and Make",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If several students share the same misconception or error during Modify, consider pausing here to bring the class back together for a quick discussion before releasing them to Make."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 1 (Modify) can be used as a formative check for combining strings and numbers using str(). Level 2 (Debug) can surface whether students can diagnose an error's cause, not just patch it. Level 3 (Make) serves as the summative check for this lesson's full objective set — combining strings, numbers, concatenation, and substrings to build an original program."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0dbe3305-b2a9-4f0e-82c5-6dcc591de84c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "470f25f4-dfca-48bd-9482-479751c85ef1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0aef04a5-12e4-4ea0-bd5f-a11b75f0665d",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0aef04a5-12e4-4ea0-bd5f-a11b75f0665d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "470f25f4-dfca-48bd-9482-479751c85ef1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e25b4b72-bdc6-4137-8f21-59aae379c60c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e25b4b72-bdc6-4137-8f21-59aae379c60c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ecc6706e-f4b5-4010-9bd2-9f9d5cd9757e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6de212df-a37b-4368-8018-d599e7197726",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the Question of the Day. Ask two or three students to briefly share what their generator program does and read its output aloud. After each share, ask the class how that student's program used both concatenation and **str()** or a substring."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6de212df-a37b-4368-8018-d599e7197726",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ecc6706e-f4b5-4010-9bd2-9f9d5cd9757e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "31f1564b-cf72-4b6d-927e-5be29fe528c8",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Teachers and students should complete the end of lesson survey.",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "31f1564b-cf72-4b6d-927e-5be29fe528c8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ecc6706e-f4b5-4010-9bd2-9f9d5cd9757e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e99b1189-1bf6-462c-9ff8-1b9372d122bc",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students predict two small expressions in order to surface that operators combine in a single line and that % is unfamiliar, without naming it yet.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *What do you think line 1 will print?*\n- *What do you think line 2 will print?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students notice line 1 didn't compute left-to-right (3*4 happened before the addition), and that line 2's % gave a result they don't yet have a name for. Don't resolve either — both get picked up later."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e99b1189-1bf6-462c-9ff8-1b9372d122bc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "717b2424-4b6a-4694-aae0-4d3e73b3e6e2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "857ef28b-3b05-4eb5-8ef5-526d25607469",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "857ef28b-3b05-4eb5-8ef5-526d25607469",
        "lesson_activity.key": "717b2424-4b6a-4694-aae0-4d3e73b3e6e2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4ff5b24b-c8f0-4c20-a474-3bdd37c6b789",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4ff5b24b-c8f0-4c20-a474-3bdd37c6b789",
        "lesson_activity.key": "717b2424-4b6a-4694-aae0-4d3e73b3e6e2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "43701e31-6136-4385-975e-acebc18689c0",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Build curiosity about round() and % through firsthand experience — predicting the math wrong, watching real inputs break the program, and noticing the leftover-cents behavior — before students ever see either function formally named or explained.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 17, Level 1. Predict the output for the given inputs, run to check, then enter \"four\" for drinks and 0 for friends to see what happens.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for whether students' predicted per_person value used floor-style thinking (786) rather than round()'s actual behavior (787). Before moving on, ask one pair what surprised them about the output",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Predict and Run",
        "progression_name": "Predict and Run"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "43701e31-6136-4385-975e-acebc18689c0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dd2e7e90-2a51-417c-b9ac-77dc202c89a4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6f874f35-9c76-4500-b741-6875302857ef",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *What did you notice about the code or the output in this program?*\n- *What do you wonder about the code or the output in this program?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should notice that the two “break it” inputs failed in different ways — typing “four” crashed before any math ran, while entering 0 friends crashed specifically on the division and modulo lines — and that the bill doesn’t split evenly, with leftover cents tracked separately from the per-person amount. Push students to wonder specifically about the round() and % lines: what is each one actually doing, and why does the program need both?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6f874f35-9c76-4500-b741-6875302857ef",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dd2e7e90-2a51-417c-b9ac-77dc202c89a4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "59160920-d6bb-4208-840c-1ab61e9a62b3",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students build an inductive understanding of % as \"what's left over,\" using the widget to test predictions before connecting it back to the seed program's own numbers.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each pair a copy of the Splitting and Rounding with Modulo handout.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Splitting and Rounding with Modulo"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "59160920-d6bb-4208-840c-1ab61e9a62b3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dd2e7e90-2a51-417c-b9ac-77dc202c89a4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ef4b55f5-5d70-4634-aea8-99e90f9b9c7e",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 17, Level 2 (Mod Clock widget) to complete Sets A–C with a partner.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for pairs rushing through Set A without checking their prediction against the widget before writing a noticing.",
        "progression_name": "Mod-Clock"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ef4b55f5-5d70-4634-aea8-99e90f9b9c7e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dd2e7e90-2a51-417c-b9ac-77dc202c89a4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c6667303-aeb3-4cea-803f-74964c47ed86",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *modulo (%)*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c6667303-aeb3-4cea-803f-74964c47ed86",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dd2e7e90-2a51-417c-b9ac-77dc202c89a4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5dc7d050-742f-4438-9a3a-aebfeb8c39d4",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *order of operations*.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Some students might misunderstand the operator precedence of multiplication and division, as in traditional math, multiplication comes first, while in Python, they are at the same level of precedence and are evaluated left to right (e.g. 4/2*3 = 6 in Python)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5dc7d050-742f-4438-9a3a-aebfeb8c39d4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dd2e7e90-2a51-417c-b9ac-77dc202c89a4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "08f181f6-5e9d-44d8-9e20-1e96b31029cd",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through to reveal the code line by line. Point out that this line has two operators of the same type, / and *, back to back. Explain that it's tempting to reach for multiplication first out of habit, but Python doesn't do that. It just reads left to right. Have students predict the output before revealing 8.0.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This is one of the most common AP exam trip-ups on tracing questions. If a student asks why the output shows 8.0 instead of 8, that's a good early preview that / always produces a float in Python."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "08f181f6-5e9d-44d8-9e20-1e96b31029cd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dd2e7e90-2a51-417c-b9ac-77dc202c89a4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aeadb754-062d-4a2e-b7e1-262281bed46d",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students formalize the order-of-operations and rounding behavior they predicted earlier, using the Trace it/Tinker/Comment it structure plus an open notes file for deeper reasoning about the program's design choices.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 17, Level 3 to complete Trace it, Tinker, and Comment it, then open the Investigate Notes file for the operator inventory and remaining prompts.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** For the Tinker step (removing str()), make sure students actually read the TypeError message before asking for help as many will assume the program is just broken rather than reading what Python is telling them.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Investigate and Modify",
        "progression_name": "Investigate and Modify",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This is also a natural moment to point out AI Tutor's Documentation and Explain Code modes are available if students get stuck reading the error."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aeadb754-062d-4a2e-b7e1-262281bed46d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dd2e7e90-2a51-417c-b9ac-77dc202c89a4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "044ab250-99af-444d-a849-7d244b3bfb04",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *Why doesn't* ***per\\_person × num\\_friends + leftover_cents*** *always equal* ***total\\_cents***?\n- _What did you notice about which **per-person** amounts printed misleadingly?_\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students articulate that round() sometimes rounds up, sometimes down, so the group's collected total doesn't always exactly match total_cents. This is a real tradeoff of rounding instead of an exact split."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "044ab250-99af-444d-a849-7d244b3bfb04",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dd2e7e90-2a51-417c-b9ac-77dc202c89a4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5ab0f5e6-c5b6-4efc-9f68-965c33a24281",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5ab0f5e6-c5b6-4efc-9f68-965c33a24281",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9179bf8b-67c5-472d-b277-2cd54c010da8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "37107e1a-295f-4a78-8d90-cea4004e03c5",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to just think about what they learned today."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "37107e1a-295f-4a78-8d90-cea4004e03c5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9179bf8b-67c5-472d-b277-2cd54c010da8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4d1e621e-ed18-4754-9a94-12c3d4abc934",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students discuss the Three W's with a partner, then cold-call one or two pairs to share out.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Listen for whether \"What did we learn\" surfaces both order of operations and modulo, not just one. For \"So what,\" push toward the real-world stakes of rounding decisions in code affecting real money, and a program's design choices (round() vs. an exact split) have consequences someone has to live with. \"Now what\" is a good opening for students to name where else they've seen rounding cause a mismatch (change at a register, splitting a bill by hand) and connect it forward to Day 2, where they'll build their own version of these tradeoffs into a program."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4d1e621e-ed18-4754-9a94-12c3d4abc934",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9179bf8b-67c5-472d-b277-2cd54c010da8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "52a4fef5-fbc5-4b22-8cf6-06e94469aa41",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "52a4fef5-fbc5-4b22-8cf6-06e94469aa41",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9179bf8b-67c5-472d-b277-2cd54c010da8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "38c55980-36a4-4a93-b850-deeebf8a128a",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Teachers and students should complete the end of lesson survey.",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "38c55980-36a4-4a93-b850-deeebf8a128a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9179bf8b-67c5-472d-b277-2cd54c010da8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6d89fba3-9929-4764-92a3-45d476c21352",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students notice that a number by itself is not always a helpful output. Students should identify that useful calculator outputs need a unit and enough context for the user to understand the result.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts. A lunch budget calculator prints the result on the right.\n- *What unit is missing?*\n- *What does the number mean?*\n- *Rewrite the output so the user understands the result.*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that 24 could mean many things unless the program explains it. Guide students toward outputs that include the unit and meaning, such as: “You need $24 for lunch this week.”"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6d89fba3-9929-4764-92a3-45d476c21352",
        "lesson_activity.key": "69142060-61c0-4b66-9f2c-972c4eceb690"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ebea23a5-6369-4cf2-823c-f622a944006c",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Use this slide to help explain how the output could be more clear. Emphasize that calculators should communicate results clearly, not just compute a number."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ebea23a5-6369-4cf2-823c-f622a944006c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "69142060-61c0-4b66-9f2c-972c4eceb690"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ba3c3158-eed8-4741-ba7b-8740e6f49d58",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When you build your own calculator today, your program should do more than print a number. It should tell the user what the result is, what unit it uses, and how to understand it.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ba3c3158-eed8-4741-ba7b-8740e6f49d58",
        "lesson_activity.key": "69142060-61c0-4b66-9f2c-972c4eceb690"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "519b8e60-e182-4ed6-8fca-34837388c2bb",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "519b8e60-e182-4ed6-8fca-34837388c2bb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "69142060-61c0-4b66-9f2c-972c4eceb690"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d071489f-00e0-49ff-bd33-465c6ab5feb3",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d071489f-00e0-49ff-bd33-465c6ab5feb3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "69142060-61c0-4b66-9f2c-972c4eceb690"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fc58d94f-eccd-4853-bb24-50567349e1f1",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students modify the existing Boba Run Splitter by adding a new money input, updating the subtotal expression, and improving the output so the group total is easier to read.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 18, Level 1. Explain that this level has two connected changes: first, students add toppings as a new cost; then, they show the final group total in dollars and cents before the split.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for whether students:\n- use float() for the toppings input\n- include toppings in the subtotal expression\n- use the rounded total_cents value when creating the group total\n- use % to find the cents portion\n- print the group total before the per-person split",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Modify: Boba Run Splitter",
        "progression_name": "Modify: Boba Run Splitter",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Frame this level as improving the calculator for the user. The toppings input makes the calculation more accurate, and the group total makes the output easier to understand before splitting the bill."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "This level can be used as a formative assessment to check whether students can add a new input, update an expression, and use operators to make calculator output clearer for the user."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fc58d94f-eccd-4853-bb24-50567349e1f1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a116f6b7-be70-470f-8ce8-c2e23bd94dc5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e937802d-fbaa-4cc5-89eb-6831fc2bda18",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** What did you discover from the modifications you made to the code?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should explain that small code changes can change both what a program calculates and how clearly it communicates the result. Adding a new input made the calculator account for another cost, and showing the group total helped the user understand the total before seeing the split."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e937802d-fbaa-4cc5-89eb-6831fc2bda18",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a116f6b7-be70-470f-8ce8-c2e23bd94dc5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a0eb3a4b-cf09-47a4-9d6a-aed9850aa254",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students plan and build a custom calculator that takes user input, uses a formula, and prints a clear result with units and user guidance.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Custom Calculator activity guide. Students should use the guide to plan their idea, inputs, formula, and output before they begin coding.",
        "duration": "25",
        "name": "Make: Custom Calculator"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a0eb3a4b-cf09-47a4-9d6a-aed9850aa254",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a116f6b7-be70-470f-8ce8-c2e23bd94dc5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "83b2da83-72a4-4d44-97dc-72cd0a725ded",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to page 3 of the activity guide. Have them review the rubric before they begin coding.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Emphasize that Convincing Evidence means the program meets the project requirements. Extensive Evidence means the calculator goes beyond the requirements by being especially clear, thoughtful, accurate, or easy for a first-time user to understand."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "83b2da83-72a4-4d44-97dc-72cd0a725ded",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a116f6b7-be70-470f-8ce8-c2e23bd94dc5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2911b672-ddc1-4ab2-b6ae-042da3d9010f",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 18, Level 2 to complete Levels 2 through 3.\n- **Level 2:** Students use AI Chat to help plan or check their calculator formula.\n- **Level 3:** Students build their custom calculator.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, listen for whether they can explain the relationship between their inputs, formula, and output. Ask questions like:\n- “Which input changes the result the most?”\n- “What unit should this result use?”\n- “How could you test this formula with easy numbers?”\n- “What will the user know after seeing your output?”\n- “Is there any input your calculator asks for but does not actually use?”",
        "progression_name": "Make: Custom Calculator",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may be tempted to choose a calculator idea before they understand the formula. Push them to keep the scope simple enough that they can explain each input, why it matters, and how it changes the result. A strong calculator is not the most complex one. It is one where the formula, inputs, and output all clearly work together."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "The custom calculator project can be used as a formative assessment to check whether students can design a calculator that takes input, uses arithmetic operators, and communicates a useful result to the user."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2911b672-ddc1-4ab2-b6ae-042da3d9010f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a116f6b7-be70-470f-8ce8-c2e23bd94dc5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "135b554f-6d65-40e8-8976-f12345b72a67",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "135b554f-6d65-40e8-8976-f12345b72a67",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a116f6b7-be70-470f-8ce8-c2e23bd94dc5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "da8330d5-6f57-42ff-a969-54c4c9cc3411",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "da8330d5-6f57-42ff-a969-54c4c9cc3411",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b1427721-9647-49f8-b06b-fa8dc3e9b816"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "80a2c692-fe90-47e3-94f6-77147b9de2f4",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to just think about what they learned today."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "80a2c692-fe90-47e3-94f6-77147b9de2f4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b1427721-9647-49f8-b06b-fa8dc3e9b816"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f20e0680-c073-4281-8900-6ca81fbe6e79",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students walk around or view shared calculator projects. As they explore, ask them to pay attention to what each calculator is designed to help the user do, what inputs it asks for, and how clearly it explains the result.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What do you notice? What do you wonder?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should see that calculators can solve many different kinds of problems, but strong calculators have a clear purpose, use inputs that connect to the formula, and explain the result in a way the user can understand."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f20e0680-c073-4281-8900-6ca81fbe6e79",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b1427721-9647-49f8-b06b-fa8dc3e9b816"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4350105a-92b0-465e-9e07-2e11463ad9c9",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Teachers and students should complete the end of lesson survey.",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4350105a-92b0-465e-9e07-2e11463ad9c9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b1427721-9647-49f8-b06b-fa8dc3e9b816"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6bb6895c-76f7-44f0-8cd0-c9b0064beebe",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students predict and collect random outcomes from a six-sided die so they can observe two key ideas: results stay within a range, and each possible result has the same chance of occurring.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each pair a copy of the Die Roll Tally Chart activity guide and a six-sided die."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6bb6895c-76f7-44f0-8cd0-c9b0064beebe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3790ff5d-fd0d-4794-ae2c-75de3ee217c2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "969475a7-7d62-40e4-b4f2-054b71b84d4b",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Before you roll, make a prediction. What is the smallest number you could roll? What is the largest? Do you think every number from smallest to largest has the same chance of showing up?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "969475a7-7d62-40e4-b4f2-054b71b84d4b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3790ff5d-fd0d-4794-ae2c-75de3ee217c2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "73ddc510-a785-4dff-9f5e-26efeb3d1bcd",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have pairs discuss their predictions first. Explain that “equally likely” means every number has the same chance of being chosen — so on a fair six-sided die, no number is more likely to come up than any other. Then have pairs roll one six-sided die 30 times and record each result in the tally chart.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students are recording each roll in the correct column and that each pair has exactly 30 total tally marks.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students do not have physical dice, they can use a virtual die (e.g. [Google Dice Roll widget](https://share.google/OxhCPeYLA87jHeeI6)). The important part is that each roll can only produce values from 1 to 6."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "73ddc510-a785-4dff-9f5e-26efeb3d1bcd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3790ff5d-fd0d-4794-ae2c-75de3ee217c2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aef3e9a9-202d-4f61-8593-f68faad24f67",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts. After pairs share, pool the class results so students can compare one pair’s data to the larger class data.\n- *Did any rolls fall outside 1 to 6?*\n- *Did every number appear the same number of times?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should notice that results may still not be perfectly even after 30 rolls, but they should usually be closer to balanced than a very small sample. No result should fall outside 1-6, and no number should be treated as “special” on a fair die."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aef3e9a9-202d-4f61-8593-f68faad24f67",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3790ff5d-fd0d-4794-ae2c-75de3ee217c2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0aa2d3e5-c098-41e5-9ccc-60368d10bcd6",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Even with 30 rolls, the results may not be perfectly even, but they should start to look more balanced. Since we used a fair six-sided die, every roll stayed between 1 and 6, and each number had the same chance of coming up. This is the idea of being ***equally likely***.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0aa2d3e5-c098-41e5-9ccc-60368d10bcd6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3790ff5d-fd0d-4794-ae2c-75de3ee217c2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "86b7a38b-9087-4cbb-94b1-815df5040379",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "86b7a38b-9087-4cbb-94b1-815df5040379",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3790ff5d-fd0d-4794-ae2c-75de3ee217c2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f3044206-6087-4903-a9aa-b5dca6411993",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f3044206-6087-4903-a9aa-b5dca6411993",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3790ff5d-fd0d-4794-ae2c-75de3ee217c2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3cfaa1cf-aa77-4ab0-8c3f-82f33c4e3887",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students connect the die roll warm-up to the key vocabulary they will use when reading and writing programs with random values.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 19, Level 1. Have students read the code and predict what numbers the program might print before pressing Run.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for students identifying 1 as the smallest possible value and 6 as the largest possible value. Encourage them to explain why the program should not print numbers outside that range.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Predict & Run",
        "progression_name": "Predict & Run",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Connect this level back to the die roll warm-up. randint(1, 6) works like rolling a six-sided die: it can choose whole numbers from 1 through 6, and both endpoints are included."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3cfaa1cf-aa77-4ab0-8c3f-82f33c4e3887",
        "lesson_activity.key": "05737ea4-06c3-4923-9b16-5d572c18fe02"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8b3b598d-40ec-4a7b-adb9-baba6e025c2e",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *What did you notice about the code in this program?*\n- *What do you wonder about the code in this program?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should notice that the program uses randint() to choose a value from a set range. Listen for students wondering how the starting and ending values affect which numbers can be chosen."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8b3b598d-40ec-4a7b-adb9-baba6e025c2e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "05737ea4-06c3-4923-9b16-5d572c18fe02"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ca5ceb69-16a3-4f75-b7e6-cf8996a4cf45",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Have students take out their copy of the CSP Unit 1 Unit Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ca5ceb69-16a3-4f75-b7e6-cf8996a4cf45",
        "lesson_activity.key": "05737ea4-06c3-4923-9b16-5d572c18fe02"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b5670e9d-41cd-47fb-956b-0c0bad47287c",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *randint().*",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Emphasize that a and b stand for the starting and ending values. In randint(1, 6), the program can choose any whole number from 1 through 6."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b5670e9d-41cd-47fb-956b-0c0bad47287c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "05737ea4-06c3-4923-9b16-5d572c18fe02"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e54d4534-3b18-43fb-bd63-13e8794ed93c",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *inclusive.* Use the image on the slide to provide an example: **randint(1,6)** includes 1 and 6.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Make sure students understand that the endpoints are included. In randint(1, 6), both 1 and 6 are possible results, just like rolling a die."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e54d4534-3b18-43fb-bd63-13e8794ed93c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "05737ea4-06c3-4923-9b16-5d572c18fe02"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3c1cb802-a889-43e6-a3cc-3dabc54a2fdf",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students investigate how randint() creates random values in code by tracing a simple program, testing it multiple times, and explaining why the output can change each time it runs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 19, Level 2. Have students run the code, trace what each line does, and add comments explaining how **randint(1, 6)** affects the output.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students explaining that randint(1, 6) stores a random number from 1 through 6 in the variable roll, and that print(roll) displays that value.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Investigate",
        "progression_name": "Investigate"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3c1cb802-a889-43e6-a3cc-3dabc54a2fdf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "05737ea4-06c3-4923-9b16-5d572c18fe02"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f17133e8-012b-41df-8ea5-1e55fc98859c",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to explain the **import** line. Emphasize that Python needs this line to access the **randint()** command."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f17133e8-012b-41df-8ea5-1e55fc98859c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "05737ea4-06c3-4923-9b16-5d572c18fe02"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1be99b80-0d3c-4e6d-a8ab-e35ae8706cce",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "**randint()** is not built into every Python program automatically. This import line gets **randint()** from the random module so we can use it in our code.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1be99b80-0d3c-4e6d-a8ab-e35ae8706cce",
        "lesson_activity.key": "05737ea4-06c3-4923-9b16-5d572c18fe02"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6b170a8f-896b-4f77-9bcb-2845a27cf99f",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to explain how random values are created and stored. Have students annotate the code snippet in their Unit Guide.\n- First, **randint()** chooses a random value within the range.\n- Then the variable stores that value.\n- Later, the program can use the variable in another command, like **print()** or **paint(red, green, blue)**."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6b170a8f-896b-4f77-9bcb-2845a27cf99f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "05737ea4-06c3-4923-9b16-5d572c18fe02"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a1bcfd17-6138-482b-95c9-7b089776a4b0",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students modify randint() ranges to create different random outcomes, then explain how the smallest and largest values affect what the program can generate.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 19, Level 3. Have students change the range to simulate a 20-sided die, test the program, then choose their own die size and explain the smallest and largest possible values.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students changing the second value in randint(1, 6) and testing the program multiple times. Check that students understand the first value is the smallest possible result and the second value is the largest possible result.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Modify",
        "progression_name": "Modify",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that changing the range changes the possible outputs. If they use **randint(1, 20)**, the program can choose any whole number from 1 through 20, including both endpoints."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a1bcfd17-6138-482b-95c9-7b089776a4b0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "05737ea4-06c3-4923-9b16-5d572c18fe02"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7ba942f5-3b72-4a5e-8825-9e0d2e75a226",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** What did you discover from the modifications you made to the code?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should explain that changing the values inside randint() changes the range of possible outputs. Listen for students to use words like smallest, largest, range, and inclusive."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7ba942f5-3b72-4a5e-8825-9e0d2e75a226",
        "lesson_activity.key": "05737ea4-06c3-4923-9b16-5d572c18fe02"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b1dd3b4d-dfcd-47b8-80c9-ea37f05990e4",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 19, Level 4 to complete Levels 4 through 5.\n- **Level 4A:** Students add a second random die roll and calculate the total.\n- **Level 4B:** Students use custom low and high values inside **randint()**.\n- **Level 4C:** Students use **randint()** to generate random bonus points.\n- **Level 5:** Students respond to a free-response question about whether **randint(1, 20)** includes 1 and 20.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students using randint() in new contexts while keeping track of the possible range. Encourage them to test more than once and explain why the output changes.",
        "progression_name": "Modify and Extend",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Emphasize that **randint()** can be used for more than dice. It can generate random scores, bonuses, ranges, colors, positions, and other values as long as students choose reasonable minimum and maximum numbers."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 5 to check whether students understand that randint(a, b) is inclusive, meaning both a and b can be chosen."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b1dd3b4d-dfcd-47b8-80c9-ea37f05990e4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "05737ea4-06c3-4923-9b16-5d572c18fe02"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fa66e34a-6fa5-4f59-9f50-94ee35169f1a",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Modify and Extend"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
